\section{Introduction}

Empirical applications of instrumental variables (IV) regressions in economics often involve multiple sets of candidate instruments. 
For example, in their seminal paper, \cite{Angrist-Krueger(1991)} studied the effect of schooling on wages by using three dummies for the quarter of birth (QoB) as IVs. Then, in pursuit of potential efficiency gain, they further interacted the three QoB instruments with states and years of birth, resulting in a total of 180 instruments (including both QoB and interacted IVs). The estimation and inference results based on the 180 IVs were reported in \cite{Angrist-Krueger(1991)} along with those based on the three IVs. 
Another example is the shift-share IVs,\footnote{See, for example, \cite{bartik1991}, \cite{blanchard1992}, \cite{adao2019shift}, \cite{Goldsmith(2020)}, \cite{borusyak2022quasi}, \cite{borusyak2025practical}, and references therein.} which have been widely applied in many fields such as labor, public, development, macroeconomics, international trade, and finance. As noted by \cite{Goldsmith(2020)}, under their identification strategy (which assumes exogenous shares), the shift-share IV can be regarded as a particular way of combining many base IVs. For instance, when estimating the inverse elasticity of labor supply, \citet[Empirical Example I]{Goldsmith(2020)} used 228 3-digit industry employment shares interacted with time period dummies to construct 684 base IVs with a sample size equal to 2166,\footnote{722 US commuting zones (CZs) with 3 time periods of 1980-1990, 1990-2000, and 2000-2010.} clustered at the CZ level. The shift-share IV takes the form of a weighted average of these base IVs. Similarly, in their Empirical Example II of estimating the (negative) inverse elasticity of substitution between immigrants and natives following \cite{card(2009)}, \cite{Goldsmith(2020)} used 38 base IVs with a sample size equal to 124. Similar to \cite{Angrist-Krueger(1991)}, the authors reported estimation and inference results based on many IVs alongside those based on the one-dimensional shift-share IV. 

%many instruments and within-cluster error dependence simultaneously. 
%The issue of many instruments and clustered data also occurs in applications with shift-share instruments, 

Given that researchers often report empirical results that are based on low-dimensional IVs, such as the shift-share IV, together with many IVs, could we combine these results in an efficient way and take advantage of the information from both sides? In this paper, we propose a combination inference procedure to solve this problem. Specifically, we consider a linear combination of three test statistics: a standard cluster-robust Wald statistic based on the low-dimensional IVs, a leave-one-cluster-out Lagrangian Multiplier (LM) statistic, and a leave-one-cluster-out Anderson-Rubin (AR) statistic. The LM and AR statistics are based on many IVs, and the leave-one-cluster-out construction is employed to remove the many-IV bias under within-cluster error dependence. %In addition,  we propose valid variance estimators that are robust to many IVs and clustered data for the LM and AR statistics. 
We first establish the joint asymptotic normality of the Wald, LM, and AR statistics and derive the corresponding limit experiment under local alternatives. Then, under the assumption that at least the low-dimensional IVs are able to strongly identify the parameter of interest, we derive the optimal combination test based on the three statistics and establish that our procedure leads to the uniformly most powerful (UMP) unbiased test among the class of tests considered. In particular, the efficiency gain from the combined test is of ``free lunch" in the sense that it is always at least as powerful as the test that is only based on the low-dimensional IVs or many IVs. Simulation experiments confirm the good performance of our combined inference procedure.

The power improvement of our combined test originates from two factors: the non-centralities of the Wald, AR, and LM statistics, and their correlation structure. 
The improvement from the correlation is important because even if the non-centralities of certain statistics among the three are zero, the statistics may still be useful, depending on the correlation structure. In particular, this implies that power improvement is possible even when the identification strength under the low-dimensional IVs differs (in terms of the order of magnitude of the concentration parameter) from that under many IVs. 
For example, suppose the shift-share IV in \cite{Goldsmith(2020)} provides strong identification for the parameter of interest while the corresponding many base IVs only provide weak identification, then power improvement over the standard Wald test (which is only based on the shift-share IV) is still possible by using our combined test, because of the correlation among the statistics. 

