\noindent
\textbf{Relation to the literature.} The contributions in the present paper relate to the literature on many (weak) instruments.\footnote{See, for example, \cite{Kunitomo1980}, \cite{morimune1983}, \cite{Bekker(1994)}, \cite{donald2001}, \cite{Chao-Swanson(2005)}, \cite{stock2005}, \cite{han2006}, \cite{Andrews-Stock(2007)}, \cite{Hansen-Hausman-Newey(2008)}, \cite{Newey-Windmeijer(2009)}, \cite{anderson2010}, \cite{kuersteiner2010}, \cite{anatolyev2011}, \cite{okui2011}, \cite{belloni2012}, \cite{carrasco2012}, 
\cite{Chao(2012)}, \cite{Haus2012},  
\cite{K13}, \cite{hansen2014}, \cite{carrasco2015}, \cite{Wang_Kaffo_2016}, \cite{Kaffo-Wang(2017)}, \cite{kolesar2018}, \cite{EK18}, \cite{solvsten2020}, \cite{CNT23}, \cite{LWZ24}, \cite{BN24}, \cite{Yap24}, among others.} 
In particular, our paper is closely related to \cite{LWZ24}.
Following \cite{Andrews(2016)}, \cite{LWZ24} proposed a jackknife conditional linear combination (CLC) test that is robust to weak identification, many instruments, and heteroskedasticity. %The CLC test also achieves efficiency under strong identification against local alternatives. 
The motivation of their analysis is the observation that, under strong identification, an orthogonalized jackknife LM test is the UMP unbiased test against local alternatives among the class of tests that are constructed based on jackknife LM and AR tests and are either unbiased or invariant to sign changes. However, the orthogonalized LM test may not have good power under weak identification or against certain fixed alternatives. Therefore, they considered a linear combination of jackknife AR, jackknife LM, and orthogonalized LM tests to ensure good power performance under different scenarios. However, \cite{LWZ24} did not consider low-dimensional IVs, so their approach cannot be applied in the current setting. Our combined test explores information from low-dimensional IVs and is robust to clustered data, while the CLC test is only robust to heteroskedasticity. 
On the other hand, while the CLC test is fully robust to weak identification, we assume that at least the IV strategy using the low-dimensional provides strong identification. Furthermore, in the context of causal inference under covariate-adaptive randomization, \cite{jiang2025adjustments} constructed a new estimator of average treatment effect (ATE) by optimally linearly combining the estimators with and without regression adjustments. 

Second, for estimation and inference with many instruments and clustered data, \cite{FLM23} considered jackknife instrumental variable (JIVE) estimation and proposed to first partial out the controls and then remove the diagonal blocks in the bilinear form of the JIVE formula, resulting in a cluster-robust jackknife IV estimator (CJIVE). %However, as carefully discussed by \cite{CNT23}, this approach suffers from an (asymptotically non-negligible) bias when there are many controls. 
In addition, \cite{FLM23} established the consistency of CJIVE under many instruments and clustering. However, they did not provide a consistent cluster-robust variance estimator or a valid inference procedure in this context. 
%This gap is important, as variance estimation in the presence of many IVs is nontrivial even under independent data and no controls. 
As pointed out by \cite{Chao(2012)}, when there are many (weak) IVs, both linear and quadratic terms contribute to the asymptotic variance, with the quadratic term potentially dominating. The conventional cluster-robust two-stage least squares (TSLS) variance estimator with the jackknifed instrument,\footnote{This one-dimensional (constructed) IV generates individual $i$'s predicted value of the endogenous variable by leaving out $i$'s own observation or $i$'s entire cluster; e.g., see Section 3.2 of \cite{chyn2024} in the case of judge design. The resulting IV estimator is numerically equivalent to JIVE or CJIVE, and thus, we need to account for both linear and quadratic terms in the variance.} such as that implemented in Stata and commonly used in empirical studies, fails to account for the quadratic term and thus becomes invalid with many IVs. On the other hand, \cite{ligtenberg2023inference} proposed cluster jackknife AR and LM tests, which are robust to weak identification, many instruments, and clustered data. 
Furthermore, it is also known in the literature that well-designed bootstrap procedures can improve the inference quality of the IV model, even under heteroskedasticity or clustering.\footnote{E.g., see \cite{Davidson-Mackinnon(2010)}, \cite{Finlay-Magnusson(2019)}, \cite{Mackinnon2021}, and \cite{Wang-Zhang2024}, among others.} Recently, \cite{lim2024dimension} proposed a dimension-robust bootstrap AR test with heteroskedastic errors, which is valid regardless of the asymptotic regime of the number of IVs. We conjecture that it is possible to extend their bootstrap AR test to the case with many IVs and clustered data. However, further modifications may be necessary to propose a valid bootstrap inference procedure for the LM test in this context. 

%pioneered by \cite{P83}, \cite{morimune1983}, and \cite{Bekker(1994)}, and further developed by \cite{Chao-Swanson(2005)}, \cite{Chao(2012)}, \cite{crudu2021}, and \cite{MS22}, among others.\footnote{\cite{anatolyev2019many} provides a comprehensive review of the literature on many (weak) instruments.} In particular,  Building on this, \cite{MS24} and \cite{BN24} applied the construction of \citeauthor{CNT23} (\citeyear{CNT23}) to develop weak-identification-robust Anderson-Rubin tests and inference for local average treatment effects with heterogeneous treatment effects under independent sampling.

%Second, \citeauthor{CNT23}'s (\citeyear{CNT23}) method relies on the assumption that there is no within-cluster dependence (after controlling for cluster fixed effects), so that deleting only the diagonal of a bilinear form can completely remove the bias induced by many IVs. Instead, we allow for within-cluster dependence and remove the diagonal blocks of the bilinear form to eliminate the bias. It is possible to modify \citeauthor{CNT23}'s (\citeyear{CNT23})  method to remove the block diagonals, as suggested by \cite{ligtenberg2023inference}. However, this involves inverting a very large matrix, which is numerically unstable and computationally demanding. Moreover, no theoretical justification has been established for this method in the literature when the data exhibit within-cluster dependence. By contrast, our inference method, coupled with the new L3CO variance estimator, is robust to both weak and strong within-cluster dependence.


Third, \cite{AD09}, \cite{K13}, \cite{EK18}, \cite{CNT23}, \cite{MS24}, \cite{BN24}, and \cite{Yap24} made important advances in estimation and inference with both many IVs and many controls. In particular, \cite{CNT23} highlighted the challenge of simultaneously partialling out all exogenous controls and removing the bias induced by many instruments. They proposed an innovative jackknife-based approach that achieves both goals and developed jackknife estimators for settings with cluster fixed effects. %Building on this, \cite{MS24} and \cite{BN24} applied the construction of \citeauthor{CNT23} (\citeyear{CNT23}) to develop weak-identification-robust AR tests and inference for local average treatment effects with heterogeneous treatment effects under independent sampling. 
In this paper, we assume a fixed number of controls and leave the setting with both many IVs and many controls as an important direction for future research. 
