\textbf{Notation.} For $n \times m$ matrix $A$ we shall use $A_{[g]}$ to denote the $n_g \times m$ submatrix of $A$, and if $m = n$ we use $A_{[g,h]}$ to denote the $n_g \times n_h$ submatrix of $A_{[g]}$. For $m \times m$ positive semi-definite matrix $A$, let $\lambda_{\max}(A)$ and $\lambda_{\min}(A)$ be the maximum and minimum eigenvalues of $A$. Let $C$ be a positive constant that does not depend on $n$, which may be different at each appearance. We denote $\left\{1, \cdots, n \right\}$ as $[n]$ and $\left\{1, \cdots, G \right\}$ as $[G]$, and abbreviate $\sum_{g \in [G]}\sum_{h \in [G]}$, $\sum_{g \in [G]}\sum_{h \in [G]}\sum_{k \in [G]}$ and $\sum_{g \in [G]}\sum_{h \in [G]}\sum_{k \in [G]} \sum_{j \in [G]}$ as $\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2}$, $\sum_{g,h,k \in [G]^3}$ and $\sum_{g,h,k,j \in [G]^4}$, respectively.  

\section{Setup and Test Statistics}\label{sec: setup}

\subsection{Setup}
We consider a clustered dataset with $G$ clusters and denote the size of the $g$th cluster as $n_g$ for $g \in [G]$. We index observations by clusters followed by units. Denote $I_g = [N_{g-1}+1,\cdots,N_{g}]$, where $N_0 = 0$ and $N_g = \sum_{g'=0}^g n_{g'}$ and $N_G =n$. Then, $\{I_g\}_{g \in [G]}$ forms a partition of $[n]$, and if $i \in I_g$, this means the $i$th observation belongs to the $g$th cluster. We then consider a linear IV regression with clustered data
\begin{eqnarray}\label{eq: IV-model}
\tilde Y_{i} = \tilde X_{i} \beta + W^{\top}_i \gamma + \tilde e_i,
\end{eqnarray}
where we denote 
$\tilde Y_i \in \textbf{R}$, $\tilde X_i \in \textbf{R}$, and $W_i \in \textbf{R}^{d_w}$ as an outcome of interest, endogenous regressors, and exogenous regressors, respectively. Further denote $\tilde Z_i \in \textbf{R}^{K}$ as the IVs for $\tilde X_i$. The first-stage equation can be written as
\begin{align*}
\tilde    X_i = \tilde \Pi_i + \tilde V_i, 
\end{align*}
where $\tilde \Pi_i = \mathbb E (X_i|\{\tilde Z_i,W_i\}_{i \in I_g})$. 

Let $\tilde Y$, $\tilde X$, $\tilde \Pi$, $W$, $\tilde Z$ be $n \times 1$, $n \times 1$, $n\times 1$, $n \times d_w$, and $n \times K$-dimensional vectors and matrices formed by $\tilde Y_i$, $\tilde X_i$, $\tilde \Pi_i$, $W_i$, and $\tilde Z_i$, respectively. We then partial out $W$ from $\tilde Y$, $\tilde X$, and $\tilde Z$. The model \eqref{eq: IV-model} can be written in the vector form as
\begin{align}
Y = X \beta + e, \quad  X = \Pi + V,
\label{eq:model}
\end{align}
where $P_W = W(W^\top W)^{-1}W^\top$ and $M_W = I_n - P_W$, $Y = M_W \tilde Y$, $X = M_W \tilde X$, $\Pi = M_W \tilde \Pi$, $e = M_W \tilde e$, and $V = M_W \tilde V$; we further denote $Z = M_W \tilde Z$. Throughout the paper, the dimension $d_w$ of $W$ is assumed to be fixed. If researchers want to include cluster-level fixed effect in the model, they can obtain \eqref{eq: IV-model} by demeaning data (outcome, controls, and instruments) at the cluster level first. The dimension $K$ of instruments is allowed to diverge to infinity with the sample size. As in \cite{Chao(2012)}, all of our assumptions are made conditional on $\tilde Z$ and $W$, and we will omit the dependence on $\tilde Z$ and $W$ in our notation whenever confusion is not possible. Let $\mathbb E$ denote the expectation conditional on $(\tilde Z,W)$, the exogeneity of $\tilde Z$ and $W$ implies $\mathbb E \tilde e_i =0$, while $\mathbb E \tilde V_i = 0$ by construction. 

Besides $K$-dimensional many IVs $Z_i \in \Re^K$, we assume that there is another set of low-dimensional IVs 
\begin{align*}
    \tilde{z}_i = f_i(\tilde Z,W) \in \Re^{d_z}, 
\end{align*}
where $\{f_i(\cdot)\}_{i \in [n_g], g\in [G]}$ is a list of known nonstochastic functions of $d_z$ dimension. Specifically, as illustrated by the example of \cite{Angrist-Krueger(1991)} in the Introduction, researchers may begin with certain low-dimensional IVs, such as the three quarter-of-birth dummies, and construct a large number of new IVs by taking the interaction between the base IVs $\tilde z_i$ and control variables $W_i$. In this case, $\tilde{z}_i$  is a subset of the $K$-dimensional many IVs $Z_i$, which include both base IVs and interacted IVs. The second example of $\tilde z_i$ is the formula IV, such as the widely used shift-share IV. 
As pointed out by \cite{Goldsmith(2020)}, under their identification strategy, the one-dimensional shift-share IV can be regarded as a weighted average of many base IVs, i.e., $\tilde z_i = \sum_{k=1}^K \tilde Z_{ik} g_k$. For instance, the employment share of industry $k$ in US commuting zone $i$ correspond to the base IVs $\tilde Z_i$, the supply shock from China to industry $k$ in US correspond to the weights $g_k$, and $K$ is the number of industries in US. 
%In general, the formula IV can be written as $\tilde z_i = f_i(\tilde Z,W)$, 
%Researchers can also construct a candidate instrument  
Let $\tilde z$ be the $n \times d_z$-dimensional matrix formed by $\tilde z_i$, and denote $z = M_W \tilde z$. 
In many empirical applications, the dimension $d_z$ is just one, but our setup also allows for $d_z>1$, while maintaining the requirement that $d_z$ is fixed w.r.t. the sample size $n$. 

We focus on the model with a scalar endogenous variable for two reasons. First, in many empirical applications of IV regressions, there is only one endogenous variable (as can be seen from the surveys by \cite{Andrews-Stock-Sun(2019)} and \cite{lee2021}). Second, as we assume either the low-dimensional IVs or high-dimensional IVs provide strong identification, our results can be extended to multiple endogenous variables by applying standard subvector inference methods, without appealing to a projection-based inference approach.\footnote{For weak-identification-robust subvector inference, in general one may use a projection approach (\citep{Dufour-Taamouti(2005)}) after implementing inference on the whole vector of endogenous variables. However, the projection approach typically leads to conservative inference. Alternative subvector inference methods for IV regressions (e.g., see \cite{GKMC(2012)}, \cite{Andrews(2017)}, \cite{GKM(2019), GKM(2021)}, and \cite{Wang-Doko(2018)}) provide a power improvement over the projection approach under a fixed number of instruments (some of these methods further require conditional homoskedasticity). However, whether they can be applied to the setting of many weak instruments is unclear. Also, \cite{Wang-Doko(2018)} and \cite{Wang(2020)} show that bootstrap tests based on the standard subvector statistic such as the AR-based statistic may not be robust to weak identification even under a fixed number of instruments and conditional homoskedasticity.}

The null and alternative hypotheses studied in this paper can be written as 
\begin{align}\label{eq: hypothesis}
	\mathcal{H}_0: \beta = \beta_0  \quad v.s. \quad \mathcal{H}_{1}:  \beta \neq \beta_0.
\end{align}
We impose the following assumptions for the data-generating process. 
\begin{ass} 
The following conditions hold almost surely for all $n$ large enough: 



\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\max_{1 \leq i \leq n} \mathbb E (\tilde e_i^6 + \tilde V_i^6) \leqslant C < \infty$;
    \item $\max_{1 \leq g \leq G} n_g \leqslant C < \infty$;
    \item Let $$\Omega_g = \mathbb E \left[  \begin{pmatrix} \tilde e_{[g]} \tilde e^\top_{[g]} & \tilde e_{[g]} \tilde V^\top_{[g]} \\ \tilde V_{[g]} \tilde e^\top_{[g]} & \tilde V_{[g]} \tilde V^\top_{[g]} \end{pmatrix}\right], \quad 1 \leq g \leq G$$
    then $$0 < \frac{1}{C} \leqslant \min_{1\leq g \leq G} \lambda_{\min}\left(\Omega_g \right) \leqslant \max_{1\leq g \leq G} \lambda_{\max} \left(\Omega_g \right) \leqslant C < \infty;$$
    \item 
    \begin{align*}
    0 < \frac{1}{C} \leqslant \lambda_{\min}\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n z_i z_i^\top \right) \leqslant \lambda_{\max}\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n z_i z_i^\top \right) \leqslant C < \infty \\ 
    0 < \frac{1}{C} \leqslant \lambda_{\min}\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n w_i w_i^\top \right) \leqslant \lambda_{\max}\left(\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^n w_i w_i^\top \right) \leqslant C < \infty
    \end{align*} and $$\max_{1\leq i \leq n} \left\{\left| \tilde \Pi_i \right| + \left|\Pi_i \right| + \left\Vert z_i \right\Vert_2 + \left\Vert w_i \right\Vert_2 \right\} \leqslant C < \infty.$$
\end{enumerate}\label{ass:reg}
\end{ass}

\subsection{Wald Statistics by Low-Dimensional IV}

It is possible to conduct inference directly based on the low-dimensional IV. 
Specifically, given a $d_z \times d_z$ positive definite weighting matrix $\hat A_n$, the GMM estimator with the IVs $z$ and weighting matrix $\hat A_n$ can be written as 
\begin{align*}
    \hat \beta_1 = (X^\top z \hat A_n z^\top X)^{-1}(X^\top z \hat A_n z^\top Y), 
\end{align*}
and the corresponding Wald test statistic can be written as 
\begin{align*}
T(\beta_0) = \frac{ \hat \beta_1 - \beta_0}{ \sqrt{\widehat \Phi_1}} = \frac{ (X^\top z \hat A_n z^\top X)^{-1}(X^\top z \hat A_n z^\top e(\beta_0))}{ \sqrt{\widehat \Phi_1}},
\end{align*}
where $\widehat \Phi_1$ denotes the cluster-robust variance estimator, and is defined as 
\begin{align*}
 \widehat \Phi_1 & =  (X^\top z \hat A_n z^\top X)^{-1} (X^\top z \hat A_n \hat \Omega \hat A_n z^\top X)(X^\top z \hat A_n z^\top X)^{-1}, \\
 \hat \Omega & = \sum_{g \in [G]} \left(\sum_{i \in I_g} z_i \hat e_i\right)\left(\sum_{i \in I_g} z_i \hat e_i\right)^\top,
\end{align*}
$\hat e_i = Y_i-X_i \hat \beta$ for some estimator $\hat \beta$ specified later, and $e_i(\beta_0)= Y_i - X_i \beta_0$. 
We make the following assumptions regarding inference with the low-dimensional IVs.
\begin{ass}
The following conditions hold almost surely: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item There exists a sequence of non-random positive definite matrices $A_n$ such that
    \begin{align*}
     A_n^{-1/2} \hat A_n A_n^{-1/2} \convP I_{d_z},
    \end{align*}
and $\lambda_{\max} (A_n) / \lambda_{\min} (A_n) \leqslant C < \infty$ for all $n$ large enough;
    \item Let $r_n = \left\Vert z^\top \Pi \right\Vert_2$, then $\sqrt{n} / r_n \rightarrow 0$;
    \item Let $P_{ z} = z A_n z^\top$ and define $\acute \Pi = P_{z}\Pi$, then $\acute \Pi^\top \acute \Pi \rightarrow \infty$ with $\max_{1\leq g\leq G} \left\Vert \acute \Pi_{[g]} \right\Vert_2^2 = o(\acute \Pi^\top \acute \Pi)$; in addition let $\lambda_{z}^{(d)}, d=1,\cdots,\bar d$ be the nonzero eigenvalues of $P_{z}$ with $1 \leq \bar d \leq d_z$, then $\max_{1\leq d \leq \bar d} \lambda_{z}^{(d)} \leqslant C < \infty$ for all $n$ large enough. 
\end{enumerate} \label{ass:low}
\end{ass}
For future reference, define  
\begin{align*}
\Phi_1 & =  (\Pi^\top z A_n z^\top \Pi)^{-1} (\Pi^\top z A_n \Omega A_n z^\top \Pi)(\Pi^\top z A_n z^\top \Pi)^{-1}, \\
\Omega & = \sum_{g \in [G]} \mathbb E \left(\sum_{i \in I_g} z_i \tilde e_i\right)\left(\sum_{i \in I_g} z_i \tilde e_i\right)^\top.
\end{align*}
In addition, define $\hat \Psi = X^\top z \hat A_n \hat \Omega \hat A_n z^\top X$ and $\Psi = \Pi^\top z A_n \Omega A_n z^\top \Pi$. With Assumption \ref{ass:reg} and $\sqrt{n} / r_n \rightarrow 0$, it can be verified that all other conditions, except for $\max_{1\leq g\leq G} \left\Vert \acute \Pi_{[g]} \right\Vert_2^2 = o(\acute \Pi^\top \acute \Pi)$, in Assumption \ref{ass:low} hold for TSLS ($A_n = (z^\top z)^{-1}$) and optimal GMM ($A_n = \Omega^{-1}$).

\subsection{Jackknife AR and LM Test Statistics}
In our setting, it is also possible to construct test statistics using the $K$-dimensional IVs (that is, many IVs). Denote $P = Z(Z^\top Z)^{-1}Z^\top$ as the projection matrix of $Z$. 
Then, the leave-one-cluster-out estimator of $\beta$ is denoted as $\hat \beta_2$ and defined as 
\begin{align*}
    \hat \beta_2 = \frac{ \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} Y_j }{ \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} X_j }, 
\end{align*}
and the corresponding test statistic can be defined if an estimator for the asymptotic variance of $\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} e_j$ is given. Such an estimator is proposed in \cite{Chao(2012)}, but here we also need to take into account the fact that $W$ has already been partialled out, whereas in \cite{Chao(2012)} the coefficients for $W$ are also estimated. Therefore, some adjustments are required, as in \cite{matsushita2024}. To that purpose, define the following symmetric matrix
\begin{align*}
    Q = M_W (P - \bar P) M_W,
\end{align*}
where $\bar P$ is the block diagonal matrix corresponding to $P$ such that the $g$-th block on its diagonal is $P_{[g,g]}$, and let $\bar Q$ be the block diagonal matrix corresponding to $Q$ such that the $g$-th block on its diagonal is $Q_{[g,g]}$. Our variance estimator is similar to the one in \cite{Chao(2012)} but with $P - \bar P$ replaced by $Q - \bar Q$, i.e., 
\begin{align*}
    \widehat \Phi_2 = \sum_{g\in [G]} \left(\sum_{h \in [G], h \neq g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \sum_{i \in I_g}  \tilde X_j Q_{ji} \hat e_i \right)^2 + \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde X_i Q_{ij} \hat e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde X_j Q_{ji} \hat e_i \right),
\end{align*} 
which is a consistent estimator of
\begin{align*}
\Phi_2 &= \mathbb E \left(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \Pi_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j + \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \Pi_i P_{ii} P_{W,ij} \tilde e_j \right)^2 + \mathbb E \left(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_j P_{ji} \tilde e_i \right)^2
\end{align*}

The test statistic of interest, the leave-one-cluster-out jackknife Lagrangian Multiplier (LM) statistic, is defined as 
\begin{align*}
    LM(\beta_0) = \frac{\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} e_j(\beta_0)}{\sqrt{\widehat \Phi_2}}. 
\end{align*}

Lastly, as pointed out by \cite{Haus2012} and \cite{LWZ24}, it is possible to use the jackknife AR statistic to further improve the efficiency of the jackknife LM statistic. In the case with clustered data, we define the leave-one-cluster-out jackknife AR statistic as 
\begin{align*}
    AR = \frac{\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \hat e_i P_{ij} \hat e_j}{\sqrt{\widehat \Phi_3}}, 
\end{align*}
where 
\begin{align*}
    \widehat \Phi_3 = 2 \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \hat e_i P_{ij} \hat e_j \right)^2
\end{align*}
is a consistent estimator of the asymptotic variance of $\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j$, given by
\begin{align*}
    \Phi_3 = \mathbb E \left(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h}  \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right)^2
\end{align*}
Here we use $\hat e_i$ instead of $e_i(\beta_0)$ to construct the AR statistic for at least two reasons: (1) the two choices are asymptotically equivalent under strong identification and local alternative, as pointed out by \cite{LWZ24} and (2) under strong identification and fixed alternative, using $e_i(\beta_0)$ can lead to non-monotonic power curve for the test combined by jackknife AR and LM statistics, as pointed out by \cite{LWZ24}, while this can be avoided by using $\hat e_i$. 
We make the following assumptions on the $K$-dimensional many IVs.
\begin{ass}
The following conditions hold almost surely:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $K \rightarrow \infty$ as $n \rightarrow \infty$ such that $\limsup_{n \rightarrow \infty}{K/n} \leqslant C < 1$ and $\sqrt{K}/\Pi^\top \Pi \rightarrow 0$;
    \item $\rank(P) = K$ and $\max_{1 \leq g \leq G} \lambda_{\max} \left(P_{[g,g]} \right) \leqslant C < 1$ for all $n$ large enough;
    \item Let $\hat \Pi = M_W (P - \bar P) \Pi = Q \tilde \Pi$ and $\bar \Pi = (Q - \bar Q) \tilde \Pi$, then 
    \begin{align*}
        \max_{1\leq g\leq G} \left\Vert \hat \Pi_{[g]} \right\Vert_2^2 = o(\hat \Pi^\top \hat \Pi), \;\;
        \max_{1\leq g\leq G} \left\Vert \bar \Pi_{[g]} \right\Vert_2^2 = o(\bar \Pi^\top \bar \Pi),
    \end{align*}
    and 
    \begin{gather*}
       \frac{1}{C} \Pi^\top \Pi \leqslant \hat \Pi^\top \hat \Pi \leqslant C \Pi^\top \Pi, \;\; 
       \frac{1}{C} \Pi^\top \Pi \leqslant \Pi^\top (P - \bar P) \Pi \leqslant C \Pi^\top \Pi 
    \end{gather*} 
    for all $n$ large enough.
\end{enumerate} \label{ass:high}
\end{ass}

\subsection{Consistent Estimation of $\beta$}

Recall $\hat e_i = Y_i - X_i \hat \beta$. The optimal combination of $(T(\beta_0), LM(\beta_0), AR)$ crucially relies on the consistency of $\hat \beta$. 
Throughout the paper, we maintain the assumption that at least one IV strategy (low-dimensional IVs or many IVs) can strongly identify $\beta$, implying that at least one of $(\hat \beta_1,\hat \beta_2)$ is consistent. However, the researchers may not know which one is consistent. Then, we will construct $\hat \beta$ as a linear combination of $(\hat \beta_1,\hat \beta_2)$ where the combination weight depends on the identification strength of two IV strategies. 

More specifically, let $\widehat \Phi_1^{pre}$ and $\widehat \Phi_2^{pre}$ be the variance estimators $\widehat \Phi_1$ and $\widehat \Phi_2$, but where the estimated errors are now constructed using $\hat \beta_1$ and $\hat \beta_2$ respectively. Our combined estimator is given by
\begin{align*}
    \hat \beta = \hat \omega \hat \beta_1 + (1 - \hat \omega ) \hat \beta_2,
\end{align*}
with
\begin{align*}
    \hat \omega = \frac{\sqrt{\widehat \Phi_2^{pre}/\left(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} X_j \right)^2}}{\sqrt{\widehat \Phi_1^{pre}} + \sqrt{\widehat \Phi_2^{pre}/\left(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} X_j \right)^2}}.
\end{align*}
The intuition of the combined estimator is that  $\Phi_1$ and $\Phi_2/\left(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \Pi_i P_{ij} \Pi_j\right)^2$ are the asymptotic variance of $\hat \beta_1 - \beta$ and $\hat \beta_2 - \beta$ under strong identification, and thus they measure the identification strengths of $\hat \beta_1$ and $\hat \beta_2$. 
$\widehat \Phi_1^{pre}$ and $\widehat \Phi_2^{pre}/\left(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} X_j \right)^2$ are their consistent estimators, respectively, under strong identification. 
If the identification for one of the two IV strategies is weak, but the corresponding variance estimator can be bounded away from zero in a proper sense, then the combination weight for that IV estimator will converge to zero, and thus $\hat \beta$ will remain consistent. Below, we show formally this combination procedure is valid of at least one IV strategy can strongly identify $\beta$.

\section{Inference with Strong Low-Dimensional IVs}\label{sec: strong low-dim}

In this section, we assume that the low-dimensional IVs are strong so that Assumption \ref{ass:low} holds. When Assumption \ref{ass:high} holds and the $K$-dimensional many IVs have the same (strong) identification strength as the low-dimensional IVs, we propose a combined test that improves the power of the Wald test that is only based on the low-dimensional IVs. Furthermore, we show that when the identification strength of the many IVs is weak, this combined test reduces to the Wald test, and thus there is no loss of efficiency. 

\subsection{Limiting Problem and Optimal Test}

If the low-dimensional IVs and many IVs have the same identification strength, we can define the local alternative based on this identification strength, which enables us to derive the corresponding limiting problem and optimal test.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:limit_str_lcl}
    Suppose there exists a deterministic sequence $d_n \downarrow 0$ such that 
\begin{align*}
& d_n \Phi_1^{-1/2} \rightarrow a_1 \neq 0,\\
& d_n \frac{ \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \Pi_i P_{ij} \Pi_j }{\sqrt{\Phi_2}} \rightarrow a_2 \neq 0, \quad \text{and}    \\
& \beta - \beta_0 = \delta d_n
\end{align*}    
for some fixed $\delta$. Assume that the following limits exist (almost surely)
\begin{align*}
    \rho_1 &= \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{\Psi \Phi_2}} \sum_{g \in [G]} \mathbb E \left[ \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \acute \Pi_i \tilde e_i \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \hat \Pi_i \tilde e_i \right) \right] \\
    \rho_2 &= \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{2}{\sqrt{\Phi_2 \Phi_3}}  \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \mathbb E \left[ \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) \right]
\end{align*} 
and $\rho_1^2 + \rho_2^2 < 1$. Then, under Assumption \ref{ass:reg}-\ref{ass:high}, we have 
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix}
    T(\beta_0) \\
    LM(\beta_0) \\
    AR
\end{pmatrix} \convD \N \left( \begin{pmatrix}
    a_1 \delta \\
    a_2 \delta \\
    0
\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}
    1 &  \rho_1 & 0 \\
    \rho_1 & 1 & \rho_2 \\
    0 & \rho_2 & 1
\end{pmatrix} \right).
\end{align*}
\end{thm}

\begin{cor}\label{cor:limit}
Suppose one observes $(\N_1,\N_2,\N_3)$, which follow the joint distribution of     
\begin{align*}
    \N \left( \begin{pmatrix}
    a_1 \delta \\
    a_2 \delta \\
    0
\end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix}
    1 &  \rho_1 & 0 \\
    \rho_1 & 1 & \rho_2 \\
    0 & \rho_2 & 1
\end{pmatrix} \right)
\end{align*}
with $\rho_{1}^2 + \rho_{2}^2 < 1$, knows the values of $(a_1,a_2,\rho_1,\rho_2)$ and the fact that $\mathbb E \N_3 = 0$, and wants to test $\mathcal H_0: \delta = 0$ v.s. $\mathcal H_1: \delta \neq 0$, then the uniformly most powerful (UMP) unbiased level-$\alpha$ test rejects if 
\begin{align*}
   \left( \frac{b_1 \tilde \N_1 + b_2 \tilde \N_2 + b_3 \tilde \N_3}{\sqrt{b_1^2 + b_2^2 + b_3^2 }}\right)^2 \geqslant \mathbb C_\alpha,
\end{align*}
where $\mathbb C_\alpha$ is the $(1-\alpha)$ percentile of a chi-squared random variable with one degree of freedom and 
\begin{align*}
\begin{pmatrix}
    \tilde \N_1 \\
    \tilde \N_2 \\
    \tilde \N_3
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
    1 &  \rho_1 & 0 \\
    \rho_1 & 1 & \rho_2 \\
    0 & \rho_2 & 1
\end{pmatrix}^{-1/2} 
\begin{pmatrix}
    \N_1 \\
    \N_2 \\
    \N_3
\end{pmatrix},
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        b_1 \\
        b_2 \\
        b_3
    \end{pmatrix}= \begin{pmatrix}
    1 &  \rho_1 & 0 \\
    \rho_1 & 1 & \rho_2 \\
    0 & \rho_2 & 1
\end{pmatrix}^{-1/2} 
\begin{pmatrix}
    a_1 \\
    a_2 \\
    0
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
\end{cor}

To implement the optimal test, we still need to estimate $(a_1,a_2,\rho_1,\rho_2)$. It turns out to be easier to estimate $\alpha_1 = a_1/\sqrt{a_1^2+a_2^2}$ and $\alpha_2 = a_2/\sqrt{a_1^2+a_2^2}$, and we propose the following estimators
\begin{align*}
    \hat \alpha_1 &= \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 + \widehat \Phi_1/\left(\widehat \Phi_2/(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} X_j)^2\right)}}, \\
    \hat \alpha_2 &= \frac{\sqrt{\widehat \Phi_1/\left(\widehat \Phi_2/(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} X_j)^2\right)}}{\sqrt{1 + \widehat \Phi_1/\left(\widehat \Phi_2/(\sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} X_j)^2\right)}}.
\end{align*}
In addition, let $\hat X = M_W (P - \bar P) X$ and $\acute X = z \hat A_n z^\top X$, for $\rho_1$ and $\rho_2$ we propose the following estimators
\begin{align*}
    \hat \rho_1 &=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\hat \Psi \widehat \Phi_2}} \sum_{g \in [G]} \left[ \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \acute X_i \hat e_i \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \hat X_i \hat e_i \right) \right], \\
    \hat \rho_2 &= \frac{2}{\sqrt{\widehat \Phi_2 \widehat \Phi_3}}  \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \left[ \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} X_i P_{ij} \hat e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \hat e_i P_{ij} \hat e_j \right) \right].
\end{align*}
The following theorem gives a feasible combined test that is asymptotically optimal among the class of unbiased level-$\alpha$ tests.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:opt_test}
    Suppose Assumptions \ref{ass:reg}-\ref{ass:high} and the assumptions in Theorem \ref{thm:limit_str_lcl} hold. Let
    \begin{align*}
        \phi_n = 1 \left\{ \left(\frac{\hat b_1 \widetilde T(\beta_0) + \hat b_2 \widetilde{LM}(\beta_0) + \hat b_3 \widetilde{AR}}{\sqrt{\hat b_1^2 + \hat b_2^2 + \hat b_3^2}} \right)^2 \geqslant \mathbb C_\alpha \right\},
    \end{align*}
    where 
    \begin{align*}
    \begin{pmatrix}
    \widetilde T(\beta_0) \\
    \widetilde{LM}(\beta_0) \\
    \widetilde{AR}
    \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
    1 &  \hat \rho_1 & 0 \\
    \hat \rho_1 & 1 & \hat \rho_2 \\
    0 & \hat \rho_2 & 1
    \end{pmatrix}^{-1/2} 
    \begin{pmatrix}
    T(\beta_0) \\
    LM(\beta_0) \\
    AR
    \end{pmatrix}
    \end{align*}
    and 
    \begin{align*}
    \begin{pmatrix}
    \hat b_1 \\
    \hat b_2 \\
    \hat b_3        
    \end{pmatrix}
    = \begin{pmatrix}
    1 & \hat \rho_1 & 0 \\
    \hat \rho_1 & 1 & \hat \rho_2 \\
    0 & \hat \rho_2 & 1
    \end{pmatrix}^{-1/2} 
    \begin{pmatrix}
            \hat \alpha_1 \\
            \hat \alpha_2 \\
            0
    \end{pmatrix},
    \end{align*}
    then
    \begin{align*}
        \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}\mathbb E \left[ \phi_n  \right] = \mathbb P \left( \left( \frac{b_1 \tilde \N_1 + b_2 \tilde \N_2 + b_3 \tilde \N_3}{\sqrt{b_1^2 + b_2^2 + b_3^2 }}\right)^2 \geqslant \mathbb C_\alpha \right)
    \end{align*}
    for $(\tilde \N_1, \tilde \N_2, \tilde \N_3)$ defined in Corollary \ref{cor:limit}, where $\mathbb P$ on the right-hand side is with respect to the distribution of $(\N_1,\N_2,\N_3)$ defined in Corollary \ref{cor:limit}.
\end{thm}

\begin{cor}\label{cor:opt}
Suppose Assumptions \ref{ass:reg}-\ref{ass:high} and the assumptions in Theorem \ref{thm:limit_str_lcl} hold. Define the following class of unbiased level-$\alpha$ tests
\begin{align*}
    \Phi_\alpha = \left\{ \begin{aligned}
        &\phi: \textbf{R}^3 \mapsto [0,1] \quad \text{the discontinuities of $\phi(\cdot)$ has zero Lebesgue measure} \\
        &\mathbb E \phi \left(\N_1,\N_2,\N_3 \right) \leqslant \alpha \quad \text{under the null} \\
        &\mathbb E \phi \left(\N_1,\N_2,\N_3 \right) \geqslant \alpha \quad \text{under the alternative}
    \end{aligned}
    \right\}
\end{align*}
where $(\N_1,\N_2,\N_3)$ is defined in Corollary \ref{cor:limit}. Let $\left\{\hat \phi_n \right\}_{n \geq 1}$ be a sequence of tests that is uniformly integrable, such that $$\hat \phi_n = \phi \left(T(\beta_0), LM(\beta_0), AR \right) + \op(1)$$ for some $\phi \in \Phi_\alpha$, then
\begin{align*}
    \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}\mathbb E \left[\hat \phi_n  \right] \leqslant \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}\mathbb E \left[ \phi_n  \right] 
\end{align*}
where $\phi_n$ is defined in Theorem \ref{thm:opt_test}. 
\end{cor}

We note that one could also analyze the case where many IVs have stronger identification strength than the low-dimensional IVs; this would correspond to letting $a_1 = 0$ in Theorem \ref{thm:limit_str_lcl}. In this case, the Wald test does not have power under the local alternative, so it is trivially less powerful than the combined test. Alternatively, it is possible that Assumption \ref{ass:high} holds but many IVs have weaker identification strength than the low-dimensional IVs, which would correspond to letting $a_2 = 0$ in Theorem \ref{thm:limit_str_lcl}. Even in this case, our combined test could still improve power when $LM(\beta_0)$ is correlated with $T(\beta_0)$; i.e., if $\rho_1$ is not equal to zero, then the combined test will be more powerful than the Wald test that are only based on the low-dimensional IVs. 

\subsection{Weak Identification of Many IVs}

We now investigate the property of our combined test when Assumption \ref{ass:high} fails. To characterize the limiting behavior of many IVs under weak identification, we introduce the following assumption. 

\begin{ass} \label{ass:high_weak}
    The following conditions hold almost surely: 
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $K \rightarrow \infty$ as $n \rightarrow \infty$ such that $\limsup_{n \rightarrow \infty}{K/n} \leqslant C < 1$;
        \item $\Pi^\top \Pi / \sqrt{K} = O(1)$ with $$\frac{1}{\sqrt{K}} \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \Pi_i P_{ij} \Pi_j \rightarrow \gamma \in \textbf{R};$$
        \item The following limits exist
        \begin{align*}
            \begin{pmatrix}
                \Gamma_{\tilde V, \tilde V} & \Lambda_1 & \Lambda_2 \\
                \Lambda_1 & \Gamma_{\tilde V, \tilde e} & \Lambda_3 \\
                \Lambda_2 & \Lambda_3 & \Gamma_{\tilde e, \tilde e}
            \end{pmatrix}
            \rightarrow 
            \begin{pmatrix}
            \Gamma_{11} & \Gamma_{12} & \Gamma_{13} \\
            \Gamma_{21} & \Gamma_{22} & \Gamma_{23} \\
            \Gamma_{31} & \Gamma_{32} & \Gamma_{33}
            \end{pmatrix}
            \equiv \Gamma > 0
        \end{align*}
        in the matrix sense and $\Gamma$ is symmetric, where
        \begin{align*}
            \Gamma_{\tilde V, \tilde V} &= \frac{1}{K} \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \mathbb E \left[ \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde V_j \right)^2 + \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde V_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_j P_{ji} \tilde V_i \right) \right] \\
            \Gamma_{\tilde V, \tilde e} &= \frac{1}{K} \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \mathbb E \left[ \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right)^2 + \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_j P_{ji} \tilde e_i \right) \right] \\
            \Gamma_{\tilde e, \tilde e} &= \frac{1}{K} \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \mathbb E \left[ \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right)^2 + \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_j P_{ji} \tilde e_i \right) \right] \\
            \Lambda_1 &= \frac{1}{K} \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \mathbb E \left[  \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde V_j \right) + \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_j P_{ji} \tilde V_i \right)\right] \\
            \Lambda_2 &= \frac{1}{K} \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \mathbb E \left[  \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde V_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) + \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde V_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_j P_{ji} \tilde e_i \right)\right] \\
            \Lambda_3 &= \frac{1}{K} \sum_{g,h \in [G]^2, g \neq h} \mathbb E \left[  \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) + \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde V_i P_{ij} \tilde e_j \right) \left( \sum_{i \in I_g} \sum_{j \in I_h} \tilde e_j P_{ji} \tilde e_i \right)\right]. 
        \end{align*}
    \end{enumerate}
\end{ass}

Note that $\Pi^\top \Pi / \sqrt{K} = O(1)$ corresponds to the case of weak identification. 
In particular, it implies that, for any test that can control size under the null regardless of $\Pi$, under any fixed alternative there exists $\Pi$ such that this test is inconsistent, as shown in \cite{MS22}. 
In our context, however, we only consider $\Pi$ such that at least one IV strategy (low-dimensional IVs or many IVs) has strong identification strength, and thus it is still possible for a consistent test to exist.

\begin{thm} \label{thm:high_weak}
    Suppose there exists a deterministic sequence $d_n \downarrow 0$ such that 
    \begin{align*}
    & d_n \Phi_1^{-1/2} \rightarrow a_1 \neq 0, \\
    & \beta - \beta_0 = \delta d_n
    \end{align*}    
for some fixed $\delta$. Under Assumption \ref{ass:reg}, \ref{ass:low} and \ref{ass:high_weak}
\begin{align*}
    \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty}\mathbb E \left[ \phi_n  \right] = \mathbb P \left( \N_1^2 \geqslant \mathbb C_\alpha \right), \quad \N_1 \stackrel{d}{=} \N \left( a_1 \delta, 1 \right).
\end{align*}
\end{thm}

Note that under the local alternative, the right-hand side is the asymptotic power of the two-sided test using $T(\beta_0)$. Therefore, provided that the low-dimensional IVs are strong, the asymptotic power of our combined test cannot be worse than that of the Wald test $T(\beta_0)$, regardless of the identification strength of many IVs.

