\section{Monte Carlo Simulations}\label{sec: simu}

We consider the following linear panel IV regression
\begin{align*}
        Y_{i,t} &= X_{i,t}\beta + W_{i,t}^\top\gamma + \alpha_i + e_{i,t} \\
        X_{i,t} &= Z_{i,t}^\top\pi + W_{i,t}^\top\delta + \xi_i + V_{i,t},
    \end{align*}
where $\alpha_i$ and $\xi_i$ are cluster-level fixed effects. The fixed effects are generated by $\alpha_i = u_{1i} + i/G, i=1, \dots, G$ and $\xi_i = u_{2i} + i/G, i=1, \dots, G$, where $u_{1i}$ and $u_{2i}$ are independent standard normal random variables. The fixed effects are partialled out from the model by demeaning at the cluster level. The control variables in $W$ are generated by standard normal distribution, and the dimension of $W$ is fixed at $d_w = 10$. The instruments in $Z$ are normally distributed with mean $0$ and we allow for cluster-level dependence: for each cluster the covariance matrix is given by $$\Omega_{1i}= \begin{bmatrix}
1 & \theta_1 & \cdots & \theta_1 \\
\theta_1 & 1 & \cdots & \theta_1 \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
\theta_1 & \theta_1 & \cdots & 1
\end{bmatrix}_{n_i \times n_i}, \;\; i=1, \dots, G,$$ and across clusters these instruments are independent from each other; we set $\theta_1 = 0.5$ in our simulation. Note that the number of clusters is equal to the number of individuals in this setting. Finally, the error terms are generated by $$\tilde{e}_{i,t} = \rho \eps_{i,t} + \sqrt{1-\rho^2} \sigma_{i,t} v_{i}$$ and $$\tilde{V}_{i,t} = \rho \eta_{i,t} + \sqrt{1-\rho^2} \sigma_{i,t} v_{i},$$ where $\eps_{i,t}$, $\eta_{i,t}$ and $v_{i}$ are independent standard normal random variables, $\sigma_{i,t} = \sqrt{(0.2+(W_{i,t}^\top\delta)^2)/2.4}$ generates heteroskedasticity, $\rho$ controls the degree of endogeneity and we set $\rho=0.5$ in our simulation. To generate more complicated error structure, we further collect $\tilde{e}_{i,t}$ and $\tilde{V}_{i,t}$ for each cluster and left multiply them by $$\Omega_{2i}= \begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & \cdots & 0 \\
\theta_2 & 1 & \cdots & 0 \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
\theta_2^{n_i-1} & \theta_2^{n_i-2} & \cdots & 1
\end{bmatrix}_{n_i \times n_i}, i=1, \dots, G,$$ to generate $e_{i,t}$ and $V_{i,t}$; we set $\theta_2 = 0.7$ in our simulation. For the parameters, we set $\beta = 0.3$, $\gamma = \delta = (1/\sqrt{d_w}) \times \iota_{d_w}$ where $\iota_{d_w}$ is a $d_w \times 1$ vector of ones. For the value of $\pi$, we consider letting the coefficient decrease geometrically with 
\begin{align*}
    \pi = \left( \phi^0, \phi^1, \cdots, \phi^{d_z-1}\right),
\end{align*}
where $\phi=\left\{0, 0.95, 1 \right\}$; note that $\phi = 0$ corresponds to the case where only the first instrument has identification strength, and $\phi = 1$  corresponds to the case where each instrument has the same identification strength. We further normalize $\pi$ to have $\left\Vert \pi \right\Vert_2 = \sqrt{\psi \sqrt{d_z}/n}$, where $\psi$ controls the identification strength.  The one-dimensional formula IV is constructed by taking the average of many IVs; as $\phi$ approaches one, the identification strength of the low-dimensional IV becomes stronger since it is closer to the optimal instrument (up to a constant). The number of observations is $n = 2,000$ and the number of clusters is $G = 500$, and we vary the cluster sizes as in \cite{Djogbenou-Mackinnon-Nielsen(2019)}: we set $\gamma = 2.0$ in their equation (21) so that the largest cluster size is $9$ and the smallest cluster size is $1$. For the dimension of $Z$, we consider $d_z = 100$ and $d_z = 500$, respectively. 

We first investigate the cases where the identification strength of many IVs is relatively strong by setting $\psi = 30$. Figures \ref{fig:1}-\ref{fig:3} show the power curves of the Wald, jackknife LM, and our combined tests for $\phi=0, 0.95$, and $1$, respectively, with $d_z=100$. 
Therefore, the identification strength for the one-dimensional formula IV is relatively weak, strong, and optimal, in Figures \ref{fig:1}, \ref{fig:2}, and \ref{fig:3}, respectively, and we observe that the power curves of the Wald test are in line with the strength of the formula IV. In addition, the power curves of the combined test is highest among the three, which is in line with our theory in Section 3. Furthermore, we note that in Figure \ref{fig:3}, when the identification strength for the one-dimensional formula IV is optimal ($\phi=1$), the power curves of the jackknife LM and combined tests overlap each other. Figures \ref{fig:4}-\ref{fig:6} show the power curves for $d_z=500$. The overall patterns remain very similar to those with $d_z=100$.
We further investigate the impact of having many weak IVs, by considering $d_z = 500$ and $\phi = 1$ so that the identification strength of formula IV is relatively strong. Figures \ref{fig:7}-\ref{fig:9} show the power curves with $\psi \in \left\{5,10,20 \right\}$. Again, we observe that in line with our theory, the combined test always has the highest power curves. In particular, the combined test has power improvement over the Wald test even when the power of the LM test is rather low (e.g., Figure \ref{fig:9}).

