\subsection{Correlation of velocity fluctuations}
\label{sec_correlation_length}


The emergence of length scales of the velocity fluctuations, gives us a measure of how correlated the motion of grains is.
Here we measure the two-point autocorrelation function of the vertical velocity fluctuations, computed with the following formula:
\begin{align}
    \tilde{C}(|\mathbf{r}|) = { \langle \delta v_y(\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{r}) \delta v_y(\mathbf{x}) \rangle 
    \over 
    \langle \delta v_y^2(\mathbf{x})\rangle} \,
\end{align}
where the average is taken over all points $\mathbf{x}$ and then over all time steps.

We observe that for all cases the autocorrelation is well fitted by an exponential decay $\tilde{C}(r) \approx e^{-r \over \ell}$ %\rt{(MT: Dimension, one to many lengths in the denominator. Probably is ($r/d_{eq}\cdot d_{eq}/l)$, or mention that $l$ is already dimensionless}, 
see figure \ref{fig:correlation}a.
