\section{The Model}
\label{sec:model}

We consider a model with a set of agents interested in buying two types of items from a single seller, where there is possibly multiple units of each item. Let $N$ be the set of agents, indexed by $i = 1, \dots, n$ where $n = |N|$. There are `weak' and `strong' item types, indexed $w$, $s$ respectively. 
%
A \emph{bundle} $\vk = (k_w, k_s)$ is a pair recording the number of units for each item. The size of the bundle is its total number of units, denoted $|\vk| = k_w + k_s$.
%
Each agent $i$ has a consumption set $\mK_i$ denoting the set of bundles it can acquire, and a valuation $v_i : \mK_i \rightarrow \reals_+$ mapping bundles to values. We assume that each $\mK_i$ contains the zero bundle and that valuations are normalized to $v_i(\mathbf{0}) = 0$. The seller's production set consists of all aggregate quantities of items it can provide, which can also be represented as bundles. For our auctions, the production set will be the set of  bundles $\vk$ such that $k_w + k_s \leq K$, where $K$ is the fixed total quantity of units the seller can provide. The items are indivisible, meaning that only integer quantities of each item can be allocated.

The \emph{prices} for the bundles are in general represented via mappings $p_i : \mK_i \rightarrow \reals_+$, expressing that the price for bundle $\vk$ to agent $i$ is $p_i(\vk)$. Note that prices can be personalized (i.e., agent-specific) in general.  In specific settings, prices typically have a particular structure (e.g., additive over items). Agents have quasi-linear utility: if agent $i$ acquires bundle $\vk$ at prices $p_i$, its utility is $v_i(\vk) - p_i(\vk)$. Agent $i$'s \emph{demand set} at prices $p_i$ is defined as $D_i(p_i) = \argmax_{\vk \in \mK_i} v_i(\vk) - p_i(\vk)$; we stress that this is a set-valued mapping, not just a selection of a single bundle.

We will refer to a subset of agents as an \emph{economy}. For an agent $i$, we write $N^{-i} = N \setminus \{i\}$. The economy $N^{-i}$ is the \emph{marginal economy} with agent $i$ removed, and $N$ is the \emph{main economy}. For notational convenience, we introduce a special index $0$ which is not associated to any agent, and we let $N^{-0} = N$. We let $N_0 = N \cup \{0\}$ and use $j = 0, 1, \dots, n$ to index the main and marginal economies.

An \emph{allocation} is a vector of bundles $(\vk_1, \dots, \vk_n)$ indicating the bundle that each agent receives. For an economy $j \in N_0$, we say that the allocation is feasible if $\vk_i \in \mK_i$ for all $i \in N^{-j}$, agent $j$ obtains the zero bundle (for $j \neq 0$), and $\sum_{i \in N^{-j}} |\vk_i| \leq K$. The seller's \emph{supply set} at prices $\vp = (p_1, \dots, p_n)$ in economy $j \in N_0$ is defined as the set of feasible allocations that maximize the seller's revenue:
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:rev-max-obj}
S^{-j}(\vp) = \argmax_{(\vk_1, \dots, \vk_n)} \left\{ \sum_{i \in N^{-j}} p_i(\vk_i) : \sum_{i \in N^{-j}} |\vk_i| \leq K, \vk_i \in \mK_i \, (i \in N), \, \mbox{$\vk_j = \mathbf{0}$ $(j \neq 0)$} \right\}.
\end{equation}
%
In an iterative auction, prices are updated in order to balance supply and demand. We next recall the classic VCG mechanism, which is a single-shot auction, and then explain how an iterative pricing mechanism can implement its outcome.

%%
%%

\subsection{VCG Mechanism}

The VCG mechanism in our setting is a sealed bid auction where the agents first communicate their valuations $v_i$ to the seller. The seller selects an \emph{efficient} allocation, which maximizes
%
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:efficient-obj}
V(N) = \max_{(\vk_1, \dots, \vk_n)} \left\{ \sum_{i \in N} v_i(\vk_i) : \sum_{i \in N} |\vk_i| \leq K, \vk_i \in \mK_i \, (i \in N) \right\}.
\end{equation}
%
Next, the auction computes and charges a \emph{VCG payment} to each agent $i \in N$, defined as:
%
\begin{equation}
\nonumber
    \vcg_i = v_i(\vk_i) - [V(N) - V(N^{-i})].
\end{equation}
%
Note that VCG payments are not unique, because they depend on the choice of the efficient allocation (which itself may not be unique). However, an agent's VCG payoff $v_i(\vk_i) - \vcg_i = V(N) - V(N^{-i})$ is fixed and does not depend on the selected allocation. Agents are not required to communicate their actual true valuations to the seller. However, it is a classic result that truthful reporting of a valuation is a dominant strategy equilibrium in the VCG mechanism~\citep{krishna2009auction}, so we have described it assuming truthful communication of each $v_i$.

Note that VCG payments are not prices: they simply define a charge for each agent based on the selected efficient allocation, whereas prices are defined over all possible bundles an agent might acquire. Comparing~\eqref{eq:rev-max-obj} to~\eqref{eq:efficient-obj}, we note an analogy between the general personalized bundle prices we have considered so far and agent valuations. However, in specific applications like multi-unit auctions and product-mix auctions, the final price structure will typically differ from structure found in agent valuations.

