\subsection{Multi-Unit and Product-Mix Settings}

The primal-dual framework can be applied to derive iterative auctions in general combinatorial auction settings with any number of indivisible items~\citep{de2007ascending,bikhchandani2002linear}. For simplicity of exposition, we have restricted our setting to just two items (`weak' and `strong'). This is enough to develop novel iterative Vickrey auctions for two important special cases: multi-unit and product-mix auctions. We first describe the latter.

\paragraph{Product-Mix Auctions.} In this setting, agents have constant marginal values for each item type, but there is a quantity constraint on the total number of units they can acquire. Specifically, agent $i$'s valuation is determined by values $(v_{iw}, v_{is})$ for the weak and strong item types, where $v_{is} > v_{iw}$, along with a quantity constraint $\capacity_i$. If $v_{iw} = 0$, this implies a constraint that the agent cannot be allocated units of the `weak' item. Agent $i$'s consumption set is therefore
%
\[
\mK_i = \left\{ (k_w, k_s) : k_w + k_s \leq \capacity_i,\, \mbox{$k_w = 0$ if $v_{iw} = 0$} \right\}.
\]
%
The agent's valuation for $\vk \in \mK_i$ is simply $v_i(\vk) = v_{iw} k_{w} + v_{is} k_{s}$. Recall that on the seller's side, the feasibility constraint for allocations is $\sum_{i \in N} |\vk_i| \leq K$. This means that on the seller's part, there is no distinction between weak and strong items. Because $v_{is} > v_{iw}$ for each agent, the efficient allocation necessarily only allocates strong items to agents, and no weak items. Following~\citep{klemperer2010product}, we will later introduce a ``supply curve'' parameter $\delta$ as a bonus towards allocating weak items, which will result in a nontrivial space of efficient allocations, but we defer this aspect until Section~\ref{sec:primal-dual-auction} where our primal-dual auction is constructed.

\paragraph{Multi-Unit Auctions.} In this setting there is just a single item type (equivalently, all agents have constant marginal value $v_{iw} = 0$ for the weak item), so we drop the $w$ and $s$ subscripts. A bundle is just a single quantity $k$ of units. Marginal values are decreasing now rather than constant: for each $i \in N$ we have $v_i(0) = 0$, and $v_i(k+1) - v_i(k) \leq v_i(k) - v_i(k-1)$ for $k > 0$. The agent's consumption set is $\mK_i = \{k : v_i(k) - v_i(k-1) > 0 \; \mbox{or $k = 0$}\}$, meaning it should not be allocated units with zero value. \citet{ausubel2004efficient} has developed an ascending-price ``clinching auction'' for the multi-unit setting which implements the VCG outcome. Our framework will provide a systematic approach for developing an iterative Vickrey auction via the primal-dual algorithm.

\medskip
For both product-mix and multi-unit auctions, the agent valuations satisfy the gross substitutes condition~\citep{baldwin2024implementing}. One implication of this property is that the LP formulations of the efficient allocation problem will have integer optimal solutions. Therefore, the LP approach will seamlessly handle indivisible items.


