\section{Subgradient Auction}
\label{sec:subgradient-auction}

In their work connecting linear programming to iterative auctions, \citet{de2007ascending} observed that applying the subgradient method, rather than the primal-dual algorithm, also leads to a procedure with an auction interpretation. The derivation is in fact quite simple, but does not lead to an auction that is guaranteed to converge to the exact optimum. Instead, one uses a fixed constant step size $\Delta > 0$, and the auction converges to prices close to the dual optimum, where the distance to the optimum shrinks with $\Delta$.

We introduce Lagrange multipliers $\rho_i(\vk)$ and $p^{-j}$ to relax constraints~\eqref{opt:primal-uce-cons-b} and~\eqref{opt:primal-uce-cons-d} in the primal UCE program, respectively. This yields, after rearranging and dropping constant terms:
%
\begin{align}
   \max_{z \geq 0, \beta \geq 0} \quad & \ds \sum_{j \in N_0} \sum_{i \in \Nj} \sum_{\vk \in \mK_i} [v_i(\vk) - \rho_i(\vk)] z_i^{-j}(\vk) \nonumber \\
   & \ds + \sum_{j \in N_0} \sum_{i \in \Nj} \sum_{\vk \in \mK_i} [ \rho_i(\vk) - |\vk| p^{-j}] \, \beta_i^{-j}(\vk) \nonumber \\
   & \ds \sum_{\vk \in \mK_i} z_i^{-j}(\vk) \leq 1,\: \sum_{\vk \in \mK_i} \beta_i^{-j}(\vk) \leq 1 \qquad \mbox{$\forall j \in N_0$, $\forall i \in \Nj$} \nonumber
\end{align}
%
The optimal solution is now straightforward to obtain. We take $\vk' \in \argmax_{\vk \in \mK_i} v_i(\vk) - \rho_i(\vk)$, or in other words $\vk' \in D_i(\rho_i)$, and for each $j \in N_0^{-i}$ we set $z_i^{-j}(\vk') = 1$ and $z_i^{-j}(\vk) = 0$ for the remaining $\vk \neq \vk'$. Note that this relies on the demand sets reported by the agents.
%
Similarly, for each $j \in N_0$ and $i \in \Nj$, we select a $\vk' \in \argmax_{\vk \in \mK_i} \rho_i(\vk) - |\vk|\, p^{-j}$. We set $\beta_i^{-j}(\vk') = 1$ and $\beta_i^{-j}(\vk) = 0$ for the remaining $\vk \neq \vk'$. Interestingly, this has a very similar form to eliciting demand sets from agents, with $\rho_i$ replacing the agent valuations. However, this part is computed purely based on the current dual solution, and does not involve actual bidding from the agents.

The subgradient update for the multipliers using this solution is then as follows, where $\Delta$ is the step size.
%
\begin{align}
p^{-j} & \leftarrow p^{-j} + \Delta \left[ \sum_{i \in \Nj} \sum_{\vk \in \mK_i} |\vk| \, \beta_i^{-j}(\vk) - K \right] \quad \mbox{$\forall j \in N_0$} \nonumber \\
\rho_i(\vk) & \leftarrow \rho_i(\vk) + \Delta \left[ \sum_{j \in N_0^{-i}} z_i^{-j}(\vk) - \sum_{j \in N_0^{-i}} \beta_i^{-j}(\vk)  \right] \quad \mbox{$\forall i \in N, \forall \vk \in \mK_i$} \nonumber
\end{align}
%
To update the offsets, we then set $\alpha_i^{-j} = \max_{\vk \in \mK_i} \rho_i(\vk) - |\vk|\, p^{-j}$ for all $j \in N_0$ and $i \in N^{-j}$. As before, the updated lower envelope prices can be communicated to the agents by transmitting the updated $p^{-j}$ and $\alpha_i^{-j}$ offsets.


