\begin{abstract}
A simple yet effective method for inference-time alignment of generative models is Best-of-$N$ (BoN), where $N$ outcomes are sampled from a reference policy, evaluated using a proxy reward model, and the highest-scoring one is selected.  
While prior work argues that BoN is almost optimal in reward vs KL tradeoffs, the effectiveness of BoN depends critically on the quality of the proxy reward model used for selection. For this purpose, we study BoN through a smooth version known as Soft Best-of-N (SBoN) and develop a theoretical framework to address this gap. We analyze the scaling behaviour of BoN by providing bounds on the KL divergence between the SBoN policy and the reference policy, offering insights into how performance varies with the number of samples. We also study the regret gap, i.e., the gap between the expected true reward under the optimal (tilted) policy and the SBoN policy. Our theoretical and empirical findings show that smoothing helps SBoN mitigate reward overoptimization, especially when the quality of the proxy reward is low.
\end{abstract}


\section{Introduction}
Large language models (LLMs) have transformed machine learning, achieving state-of-the-art results on a variety of tasks. Despite all advancements, LLMs can still generate undesirable outputs, such as toxic or factually incorrect responses. This has made alignment a central goal in modern LLM development \citep{achiam2023gpt,team2023gemini}.

Several post-hoc alignment methods have been proposed to address this challenge, including Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) \citep{christiano2017deep,ouyang2022training}, SLiC \citep{zhaocalibrating}, Direct Preference Optimization \citep{rafailov2023direct}, controlled decoding \citep{mudgal2024controlled} and Best-of-N (BoN) sampling \citep{beirami2024theoretical}. While these methods differ in their implementation—ranging from training-time optimization to test-time selection—they can be viewed, in principle, as approximating the solution to a KL-regularized reward maximization problem.  The optimal solution to this problem is a tilted distribution over responses, which balances reward and proximity to the reference model \citep{yang2024asymptotics}. 

In BoN as a test-time sampling strategy, given a prompt, $N$ responses are sampled from the reference policy, and the one with the highest estimated reward is selected.  Empirically, BoN has been shown to achieve competitive or superior performance in the reward-versus-KL divergence trade-off when compared to RLHF and other alignment methods \citep{gao10256836framework,mudgal2024controlled} under \emph{true reward model}. Furthermore, under certain conditions, it asymptotically approximates the solution to the KL-regularized reward maximization objective \citep{yang2024asymptotics}. However, in practice, BoN relies on a learned proxy reward model—an approximation of the true reward function—to guide this selection. As such, their effectiveness critically depends on both the \emph{proxy reward model} (estimation error of true reward) and the \emph{quality of the reference policy}.

Understanding how these two components, the quality of the proxy reward model and the choice of reference policy, affect the alignment quality of test-time sampling algorithms is essential. There are different measures of alignment quality, including KL divergence \footnote{Unless stated otherwise, all KL divergences are understood to be measured between the aligned policy and the reference policy.} between aligned policy and reference policy and the \emph{regret} defined as the gap between the expected true reward under the optimal (tilted) policy and the alignment policy. Note that minimizing the regret gap is critical to ensuring high-quality outputs and close performance to the optimal policy. Recent work by \citet{gao10256836framework} and \citet{hilton2022measuring} has investigated the scaling laws governing reward model optimization in both reinforcement learning (RL) and BoN settings as a function of KL divergence between aligned policy and reference policy. They empirically demonstrate that, under proxy reward models, the improvement in expected true reward, relative to a reference policy, scales proportionally for both RL and BoN policies.

While recent work analyzes BoN under the idealized settings where there is no discrepancy between the proxy reward and the true reward~\citep{yang2024asymptotics,beirami2024theoretical,mroueh2024information,huang2025best}, our work relaxes this assumption to study the interplay between the reward discrepancy measured through regret and the $\mathrm{KL}$-divergence.
We present a theoretical study of \textbf{Soft Best-of-$N$ (SBoN)}, a smoothed variant
of BoN recently introduced by \citet{Flavio2025SoftBoN,jinnai2024regularized}.
Unlike BoN, SBoN draws the final response \emph{probabilistically} from
the $N$ candidates, yielding a policy that is
 tunable with a temperature parameter.
Our analysis centres on two metrics:

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
    \item the Kullback–Leibler divergence between SBoN policy (under the \emph{true} reward or proxy reward model) and the reference policy, and
    \item the \emph{regret}, i.e.\ the expected true reward gap between optimal policy and SBoN policy.
\end{enumerate}
We show how these results specialize to the BoN (as a limit of SBoN for the temperature goes to infinity) and quantify the estimation error
incurred by using a \emph{proxy} reward model instead of the true reward.
Finally, we characterize regimes in which SBoN attains \emph{lower} regret bound than BoN when we use the proxy reward model.

Our main contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item We derive finite-sample bounds for KL divergence between the SBoN policy and reference policy, and for the regret gap of the SBoN policy, and we extend these bounds to BoN. These bounds reveal how the number of responses
$N$, proxy reward model quality and reference policy model affect performance.
    \item We quantify cases where SBoN performs better than BoN under overoptimization scenario where the proxy reward model is used instead of the true reward model.
    \item We provide experimental validation using various proxy reward models to demonstrate SBoN's advantages in the overoptimization scenario.
\end{itemize}



\section{Related Works}\label{sec:related_works}
In this section, we discuss related works on BoN, the theoretical foundation of (Soft) BoN and overoptimization. More related works for the theoretical foundation of RLHF and smoothing of maximum are provided in the Appendix (App)~\ref{app:related_works}.

\textbf{Best-of-N:} Despite many recent advancements in alignment, a simple, popular, and well-performing method continues to be the BoN policy~\citep{nakano2021webgpt,stiennon2020learning, beirami2024theoretical}. In fact, \citet{gao2023scaling, mudgal2024controlled, eisenstein2023helping} show that BoN consistently achieves compelling win rate–KL tradeoff curves, often outperforming KL-regularized reinforcement learning and other more complex alignment strategies. LLaMA 2~\citep{touvron2023llama} leverages BoN outputs as teacher signals to further finetune the base model. \citet{mudgal2024controlled} extend BoN through 
Q-learning to block-wise BoN decoding.
This empirical effectiveness has also inspired research into distilling BoN behaviour into standalone models~\citep{ amini2024variational, sessa2024bond, qiu2024treebon}. 
\citet{hughes2024best} utilize BoN as an effective method for jailbreaking, while BoN is also commonly used as a strong baseline for scaling inference-time compute~\citep{brown2024large,snell2024scaling}. Given the broad success of BoN, we are motivated to theoretically investigate the BoN policies and the effect of the proxy reward model (reward hacking) and the quality of the reference policy.


\textbf{Theoretical Foundation of (Soft) BoN:} KL divergence of BoN is studied in \citep{beirami2024theoretical,mroueh2024information} via information theoretical tools where the KL divergence of BoN sampling from the reference distribution is bounded by $\log(N)-(N-1)/N$. 
Scaling laws governing reward as a function of KL divergence is empirically studied by \citet{gao2023scaling} and theoretically formalized by~\citet{mroueh2024information}.
Furthermore, the asymptotic case and the equivalence of
BoN to the KL-constrained reinforcement learning solution are studied by \citet{yang2024asymptotics} under the assumption of access to optimal reward. \citet{gui2024bonbon} further characterized the win rate–KL gap in the asymptotic regime where a model assigns extremely low likelihoods to successful completions. Furthermore, \citet{sunfast2024} accelerated BoN using speculative rejection sampling. The regret of BoN under some assumptions is studied in \citep{huang2025best}. The convergence rate of the SBoN policy to the optimal tilted policy has been analyzed by \citet{Flavio2025SoftBoN}. Additionally, \citet{geuter2025guided} investigate a variant of SBoN that incorporates speculative samples from a small auxiliary model, providing both theoretical and empirical insights. However, the regret gap and KL divergence of SBoN under overoptimization scenario remain largely unexplored in the existing literature.

\textbf{Overoptimization.} Alignment methods are widely known to suffer from overoptimization, also known as misspecification, reward hacking, or Goodhart Law, where optimizing against a proxy reward model leads to degraded performance compared to the true reward model \citep{amodei2016concrete, casper2023open, gao2023scaling}. 
This issue is particularly pronounced in inference-time alignment methods such as BoN, where an increasing number of responses $N$ makes the overoptimization problem worse \citep{huang2025best, stroebl2024inference, gao2023scaling}. \citet{huang2025best} theoretically demonstrate that the BoN policy suffers from overoptimization when $N$ is large, given a fixed estimation error in the reward model, and propose a solution based on a $\chi^2$-regularized framework. Other approaches to mitigating this issue include ensembling strategies \citep{costereward,eisensteinhelping} and regularization techniques \citep{ichiharaevaluation}. In a concurrent line of work, \citet{khalaf2025inference} introduce the Best-of-Poisson method to reduce overoptimization in inference-time algorithms. The overoptimization in BoN and SBoN is also studied by \citet{khalaf2025inference} and a principled hedging framework is proposed to mitigate the overoptimization. In contrast, we study overoptimization in inference-time alignment methods SBoN and BoN from the perspectives of regret gap and KL divergence analysis.


\section{Problem Formulation}\label{sec:problem-formulation}

\textbf{Notations:} Upper-case letters denote random variables (e.g., $Z$), lower-case letters denote the realizations of random variables (e.g., $z$), and calligraphic letters denote sets (e.g., $\mathcal{Z}$). 
All logarithms are in the natural base. The set of 
probability distributions (measures) over a space $\mathcal{X}$ with finite variance is denoted by $\mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})$. The KL divergence between two probability distributions on $\mathbb{R}^d$ with densities $p(x)$ and $q(x),$ such that $q(x) > 0$ when $p(x) > 0$, is $\KLr(p\|q) := \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} p(x)\log(p(x)/q(x))\mrd x$ (with $0.\log 0:=0$). The total-variation distance is defined as $\mathbb{TV}(p,q)=\frac{1}{2}\int_{\mathcal{X}}|p(x)-q(x)|$. Furthermore, we define chi-square divergence as $\chi^2(p(x)\|q(x))=\int_{\mathcal{X}}\frac{p^2(x)}{q(x)}-1$.


 \subsection{Preliminaries} Following \citet{ye2024theoretical} and \citet{zhao2024sharp}, we define
inference-time alignment as an entropy-regularized optimization problem.
Let the finite set of prompts be $\mathcal{X}$ and the discrete finite set of responses be
$\mathcal{Y}$. Prompts are drawn from a distribution
$\rho$ over $\mathcal{X}$.  A (stochastic) policy
$\pi\!\in\!\Pi$ assigns, for every prompt $x\!\in\!\mathcal{X}$, a conditional
distribution $\pi(\cdot\mid x)$ over $\mathcal{Y}$; drawing
$y\!\sim\!\pi(\cdot\mid x)$ yields a response.

We treat the supervised-fine-tuned (SFT) model as a
\emph{reference policy}, denoted $\pirefer(\cdot\mid x)$.
Given a true reward function
$\rstar\!:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}\!\to\!\mathbb{R}^+\cup 0$ and temperature
$\beta>0$, we seek a policy that remains close to $\pirefer$ while maximizing
expected true reward, leading to the KL-regularized objective
\begin{equation}\label{eq:kl_objective}
\begin{split}
     \max_{\pi\in\Pi}\;
   &\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim\pi(\cdot\mid x)}\!\bigl[\rstar(x,Y)\bigr]
  \\&\quad \;-\;\frac{1}{\beta}\;
        \KLr\!\bigl(\pi(\cdot\mid x)\,\|\,\pirefer(\cdot\mid x)\bigr).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
The unique solution is the \emph{tilted} optimal policy
\citep{korbak2022rl,korbak2022reinforcement,yang2024asymptotics}
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\pibetarstar\big(y|x\big)
   \;=\;
   \frac{
     \pirefer(y\mid x)\,
     \exp\!\bigl(\beta\,\rstar(x,y)\bigr)}%
     {\zstar},
   \\
  &\quad\zstar=
     \sum_{y\in\mathcal{Y}}\pirefer(y\mid x)\,
     \exp\!\bigl(\beta\,\rstar(x,y)\bigr),
     \end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\zstar$ is the normalizing (\emph{partition}) function. 

Note that, in practice, we do not have access to the closed form of reference policy $\pirefer(y|x)$ and $\rstar(y,x)$. We can only first estimate the true reward function via proxy reward function $\rhat(y,x)$ using some datasets and then sample from $ \pirefer(y|x)$ and compute $\rhat(y,x)$ for each individual sample. Then, for alignment, we can apply inference time algorithms, e.g., BoN or SBoN~\citep{Flavio2025SoftBoN}, where $N$ samples are generated from $ \pirefer(y|x)$ and we choose the sample with the highest estimated reward (BoN) or sampled from a distribution (SBoN) using the estimated reward, $\rhat(y,x)$. When only a proxy reward $\rhat$ is available, we obtain the analogous partition function $\zhat$ and policy $\pibetarhat(\cdot|x)$.

\subsection{SBoN Algorithm}
In SBoN, as introduced in \citep{Flavio2025SoftBoN}, the sampling strategy includes,
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Choose $N$ i.i.d. responses, $Y_1,\cdots,Y_n$, for a given prompt $x\in\mathcal{X}$ from $ \pirefer(y|x)$,
    \item Draw $Z$ from $\{1,\cdots,N\}$ with softmax distribution with temperature $\beta$, 
    \begin{equation}
        \mathrm{Pr}(Z=i)=\frac{\exp(\beta\rhat(Y_i,x))}{\sum_{j=1}^N \exp(\beta\rhat(Y_j,x))},
    \end{equation}
    \item Return $Y_{\mathrm{SBoN}}:=Y_Z$.
\end{enumerate}
We denote the final policy from SBoN via $\pisbon(y|x)$. Note that for $\beta\rightarrow\infty$ and $\beta\rightarrow-\infty$, we recover BoN and worst-of-N (WoN)~\citep{balashankar2024infalign}, respectively. Furthermore, for $\beta\rightarrow 0$, we recover uniform sampling among the $N$ response samples, which is equivalent to sampling from the reference model $\pirefer(y|x)$. In \citep[Lemma~1]{Flavio2025SoftBoN}, the closed form solution of SBoN is derived,
\begin{equation}
    \pisbon(y|x)= \frac{\pirefer(y|x) \exp(\beta \rhat(y,x))}{Z_{N,\beta}},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
     &Z_{N,\beta}=\\&\mathbb{E}\Big[\Big( \frac{1}{N}\big(\exp(\beta \rhat(y,x))+\sum_{i=1}^{N-1}\exp(\beta \rhat(Y_i,x))\big)\Big)^{-1}\Big]^{-1}.
\end{split}
\end{equation}

Similarly, we can define $\pisbontrue(y|x)$ based on a true reward model. For simplicity, we define BoN policies under true and proxy reward models as $\pibontrue(y|x)$ and $\piboninfty(y|x)$, respectively.
In this work, we focus on $\beta \geq 0$. Another motivation for SBoN based on the Gumbel-Max trick is provided in Appendix (App)~\ref{app:gumble}.



\subsection{Assumptions}
For our analysis, the following assumptions are needed.
\begin{assumption}[Bounded True Reward]\label{ass:bounded_reward}
We assume that the true and estimated reward functions, $\rstar(y,x)$ and $\rhat(y,x)$, are non-negative functions, bounded by $R_{\max}$.
\end{assumption}
Bounded reward assumption is also considered in previous work  \citep{huang2025best} for BoN. Furthermore, calibrated rewards, as introduced by \citep{beirami2024theoretical,balashankar2024infalign}, are bounded. We can relax this assumption using a sub-gaussian or sub-exponential reward function as discussed in \citep{mroueh2024information}.

Let's define the tilted average of square estimation error of reward function for a given prompt $x$ with parameter $\beta$, as follows,
\begin{equation}
    \varepsilon_{\beta,r}(x) : = \frac{1}{\beta}\log\Big(\mathbb{E}_{Y\sim \pirefer(y|x)}[e^{\beta(\rstar(Y,x)-\rhat(Y,x))^2}]\Big).
\end{equation}
A similar definition of estimation error is introduced in \citep{yang2022convergence}. When $\beta = 0$, the definition reduces to the mean-squared error, which is also introduced in \citep{huang2025best}. Letting $\beta \to \infty$ recovers the square of the supremum (infinity) norm of the estimation error between $\rstar(y,x)$ and $\rhat(y,x)$. The tilted average of the estimation error is monotonically increasing in $\beta$.

In addition to Assumption~\ref{ass:bounded_reward}, for regret analysis the following assumptions are needed.
\begin{assumption}[Bounded Estimation Error]\label{ass:bounded_est_reward}
    We assume that estimation error for reward function for all $x\in\mathcal{X}$ is bounded, i.e., $ \varepsilon_{\beta,r}(x)<\infty$.
\end{assumption}
In practice, the estimated reward can be fitted to a human-preference labeled dataset or a dataset with samples from true rewards. As discussed in \citep{huang2025best}, for simplicity, we just assume that $\rhat(y,x)$ is given, where its estimation error with respect to the true reward function under reference policy is bounded. 


\begin{assumption}[Achievable maximum reward]\label{ass:achieve_reward}
    We assume that $\rhat(\hat{y}(x),x)=R_{\max}$ for all $\hat{y}(x)\in \hat{\mathcal{Y}}(x)$. 
\end{assumption}
In many settings, the reward function attains its maximum at specific responses. In particular, since a large language model (LLM) generates outputs using a finite vocabulary and a bounded number of tokens, the space of possible generations is finite, and thus the assumption holds trivially. Next, we define overoptimization. 
\begin{definition}[Overoptimization]
    The overoptimization happens whenever we have $ \varepsilon_{\beta,r}(x)>0$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Optimal Policy and Coverage}
We define the optimal policy under the true reward model as,\begin{equation}\label{eq:pistar}\pi^{\star}_{\rstar}(y|x)=\arg\max_\pi \mathbb{E}_{Y\sim\pi(\cdot|x)}[\rstar(Y,x)].\end{equation}
Similarly, we can define $\pi^{\star}_{\rhat}(y|x)$ as the optimal policy under the proxy reward model.

Under bounded reward assumption, Assumption~\ref{ass:bounded_reward}, we can interpret optimal policies as the limit of tilted optimal policies,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:asy_pi}
\begin{split}
\pi_{\infty,\rstar}(\cdot|x) &:= \lim_{\beta \rightarrow \infty} \pibetarstar(\cdot|x), \\
\pi_{\infty,\rhat}(\cdot|x) &:= \lim_{\beta \rightarrow \infty} \pibetarhat(\cdot|x).
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $\pi_{\infty,\rstar}(\cdot|x)$ and $\pi_{\infty,\rhat}(\cdot|x)$ place all their probability mass on the maximizers of $\rstar(y,x)$ and $\rhat(y,x)$, respectively. Therefore, under bounded reward assumption, we have $\pi^{\star}_{\rstar}\big(\cdot|x\big)=\pi_{\infty,\rstar}(\cdot|x)$ and $\pi^{\star}_{\rhat}\big(\cdot|x\big)=\pi_{\infty,\rhat}(\cdot|x)$. The connections between optimal, SBoN, BoN and tilted optimal policies under true or proxy reward models are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:policies_true}. 

