Some of these examples may appear to have similar community structures, as one can be transformed into another by adding or flipping one arrow in their motif diagrams (Fig.~\ref{fig:motifs}B). However, they reveal qualitatively different dynamics in terms of how far they are from equilibrium (as will be quantified later). We use Maximum Caliber to model all these community structures and to assess the ability of Maximum Caliber to correctly infer the driving parameters of the model.



\section*{Results}

\subsection*{Accuracy of parameter inference}

Maximum Caliber is particularly useful because it provides a method for inferring the parameters of the occupancy model.  An important concern is the degree to which such inferences are precise and unbiased.  We tested the inference of parameters using logistic regression from simulation data over a broad array of simulations that varied the number of patches, the number of species, and the duration of the time series. To do so, we simulated data resulting from selecting the parameters $\eta_{ij}$ and $\lambda_i$ subject to the assumptions of each motif, and then we used logistic regression on the simulation data to estimate the parameter values $\hat{\eta}_{ij}$ and $\hat{\lambda}_i$.  We next compared these estimated parameters with the original values $\eta_{ij}$ and $\lambda_i$ used to generate the simulations. We tested the accuracy of parameter inference using the random interactions model (the choice of parameters is described in the appendix); the other motifs yielded similar results.

To quantify the accuracy of our inferences, we compared the estimated values of the parameters against the original values by linear regression. The slope and intercept of the regression  indicates whether there is bias in the parameter inference, and the residual variance measures how accurate we can expect any particular parameter estimation to be. Examples of such comparisons are shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:inf_exam}. The slopes and intercepts are very close to 1 and 0 respectively, and the residual variances are small.  Crucially, these inferences appear equally good for all the parameters and indicate no bias in the estimates.

We expect the accuracy of our inference to depend positively on the length of the time series ($T$) and negatively on the system size (the number of interconnected patches $M$ and the number of species $N$). Plots of how these measures of accuracy vary as the length of the time series, number of species, and number of patches change are shown in Fig. \ref{fig:inf_exam}.  These figures confirm our expectations and provide a guide as to how many data points are needed for estimating parameters in applications to real systems (see Discussion).

