\section*{Appendix}

\subsection*{Derivation of the Maximum Caliber solution}

To maximize the function in Eq.~(\ref{eq:Lagrange}), we set its derivative with respect to $P(\Gamma)$ equal to zero:
\begin{align}
\frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial P(\Gamma)} = - \log P(\Gamma) - 1 + \sum_{ij} \eta_{ij} \, \frac{1}{T} \sum_t x_i^{(t+1)} x_j^{(t)} + \sum_i \lambda_i \, \frac{1}{T} \sum_t x_i^{(t)} + \mu = 0 .
\end{align}
Solving for $P(\Gamma)$ gives
\begin{align} \label{eq:P_Gamma}
P(\Gamma) = \exp \left( \frac{1}{T} \sum_t \left( \sum_{ij} \eta_{ij} \, x_i^{(t+1)} x_j^{(t)} + \sum_i \lambda_i \, x^{(t)}_i \right) + \mu - 1 \right) .
\end{align}
The parameter $\mu$ can be fixed by the normalization of probability, yielding:
\begin{equation}
\mu = 1 - \log \left( \sum_{\Gamma} \exp \left( \frac{1}{T} \sum_t \left( \sum_{ij} \eta_{ij} \, x_i^{(t+1)} x_j^{(t)} + \sum_i \lambda_i \, x^{(t)}_i \right) \right) \right) .
\end{equation}
Recall that $P(\Gamma)$ is the probability of a trajectory, $\Gamma = \{ \mathbf{x}^{(1)} \cdots \mathbf{x}^{(T)} \}$. The form of Eq.~(\ref{eq:P_Gamma}) implies that the process is Markovian \citep{ge:2012}, i.e., $P \big( \mathbf{x}^{(t+1)} \big| \mathbf{x}^{(1)} \cdots \mathbf{x}^{(t)} \big) = P \big( \mathbf{x}^{(t+1)} \big| \mathbf{x}^{(t)} \big)$ (see detailed proof in Supplementary Material). Therefore, we can rewrite the probability of a trajectory as a product of conditional probabilities: 
\begin{equation}
P(\Gamma) \equiv P \big( \mathbf{x}^{(1)} \cdots \mathbf{x}^{(T)} \big) = P \big( \mathbf{x}^{(1)} \big) \prod_{t=1}^{T-1} P \big( \mathbf{x}^{(t+1)} \big| \mathbf{x}^{(t)} \big) .
\end{equation}
It can be shown that the conditional probability for each time step is given by: 
\begin{align} \label{eq:cond-prob}
P \big( \mathbf{x}^{(t+1)} \big| \mathbf{x}^{(t)} \big) = \frac{1}{Z_t} \exp \left( \sum_{ij} \tilde{\eta}_{ij} \, x_i^{(t+1)} x_j^{(t)} + \sum_i \tilde{\lambda}_i \, x^{(t+1)}_i \right) ,
\end{align}
where $\tilde{\eta}_{ij}$ and $\tilde{\lambda}_i$ are parameters defined in terms of the original parameters $\eta_{ij}$ and $\lambda_i$ (see Supplementary Material), and $Z_t$ is a normalization factor. We can further write Eq.~(\ref{eq:cond-prob}) as a product of conditional probabilities for each species, i.e., $P \big( \mathbf{x}^{(t+1)} \big| \mathbf{x}^{(t)} \big) = \prod_i P \big( x_i^{(t+1)} \big| \mathbf{x}^{(t)} \big)$ with
\begin{equation}
P \big( x_i^{(t+1)} \big| \mathbf{x}^{(t)} \big) = \frac{1}{Z_{t,i}} \exp \left( x^{(t+1)}_i  \left( \sum_{j} \tilde{\eta}_{ij} \, x_j^{(t)} + \tilde{\lambda}_i \right) \right) .
\end{equation}
Fixing the normalization factor $Z_{t,i}$ (and redefining the parameters by dropping the tilde) leads to the result in Eq.~(\ref{eq:cond-prob-1}).


\subsection*{Choice of parameter values}

In the production of data used to produce Fig.\ref{fig:inf_exam} the $\eta_{ij}$ on the main diagonal (green in Fig. \ref{fig:motifs}A) are taken from a uniform distribution from 0 to 0.5. The diagonal of each on diagonal block is made identical so that the baseline persistence parameters are only species specific. This ensures that a species is more likely to be present at the next time step, if it is present in the current time step. The $\eta_{ij}$ in the main diagonal blocks but away from the main diagonal (blue in Fig. \ref{fig:motifs}A) are taken from a normal distribution with mean 0 and a standard deviation of .5. To assign the values of $\eta_{ij}$ that are associated with migration (red in Fig. \ref{fig:motifs}A), first, each pair of patches is either connected or not, with a probability of $1/M$. For each pair of patches that are connected, the diagonal of the block associated with that pair is filled with parameters chosen from a uniform distribution from 0 to 1. The $\lambda_i$ are assigned per-species taken from a normal distribution with mean 0 and standard deviation of 1. Additionally , to include environmental variation between patches, an additional random factor is added to each $\lambda_i$ that is taken from a normal distribution centered at 0 with standard deviation of .2. The extra factor means that the same species in different patches have similar but not identical interactions with the abiotic factors in their environment.

In the production of data used to produce Fig. \ref{fig:ent_r2}, the parameters are chosen in accordance with each systems associated motif. Each system has 3 species and 3 patches and the patches are all connected. For the random interaction motif (red in Fig. \ref{fig:motifs}B) the parameters are chosen in the same manner as above except without the environmental variation added to the $\lambda_i$. In the other motifs, the $\eta_{ij}$ and the $\lambda_i$ are set to 1. For the apparent competition motif (yellow in Fig. \ref{fig:motifs}B) interspecific interaction parameters (blue in Fig. \ref{fig:motifs}A), a strength of interaction $s$ is chosen and then in each on diagonal block, $\eta_{0 2}=\eta_{12}=-s$, $\eta_{20}=s$, and $\eta_{21}=s/2$. For the intraguild competition motif (green in Fig. \ref{fig:motifs}B) three strengths are chosen $a$, $b$, and $c$ and then, in each diagonal block $\eta_{20}=-\eta_{02}=a$, $\eta_{21}=-\eta_{12}=b$, and $\eta_{10}=-\eta_{01}=c$. For the non-transitive competition motif (purple in Fig. \ref{fig:motifs}B) a strength $s$ is chosen and then in each diagonal block $\eta_{01}=\eta_{12}=\eta_{20}$=-s. All of theses parameter choices ensure that each predator has a negative relationship with the presence of its prey and each prey has a positive effect on the presence of its predator.

