\section{Introduction}
Transcriptome profiling offers a molecular-level lens into human disease, accelerating drug discovery by enabling detailed insights into gene expression and cellular function. It supports the identification of novel therapeutic targets and the development of personalized treatments\cite{casamassimi_transcriptome_2017}. This field encompasses both bulk RNA sequencing (bulk RNA-seq), which provides a comprehensive overview of gene expression profiles in cell populations, facilitating comparative transcriptomics, biomarker discovery, and functional genomics, and single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq), which offers finer resolution by capturing gene expression at the individual cell level. scRNA-seq enables the study of cell-type-specific gene expression, exploration of cellular heterogeneity, identification of rare cell populations, and tracking of antigen-specific responses\cite{li_bulk_2021}. 

With the exponential growth of publicly available transcriptomic datasets, integrative approaches that combine diverse sources now offer a more comprehensive understanding of biological systems\cite{angerer_single_2017}. These methods often leverage machine learning and deep learning to integrate data and uncover hidden patterns across studies\cite{cheng_machine_2024}. Meanwhile, the field of machine learning itself has undergone a significant paradigm shift, moving away from models designed for specific, well-defined problems towards general-purpose \emph{foundation models} trained on large-scale data\cite{bommasani_opportunities_2022}, yielding breakthroughs in natural language processing (NLP), understanding, and computer vision\cite{zhou_comprehensive_2024,awais_foundation_2025}. Inspired by these advancements, a new wave of foundation methods aims to decode the language of biology by leveraging genomics, transcriptomics, proteomics,  and epigenomics at scale\cite{si_foundation_2024}.  Among these, single-cell transcriptomic foundation models (TFMs) stands out for their ability to capture dynamic, cell-specific gene expression patterns, and provide deeper insights into gene regulatory relationships, cellular functions, and real-time biological processes. 

TFMs are based on the transformer architecture\cite{vaswani2017attention}, which excels at capturing long-range dependencies, making it well-suited for analyzing complex relationships between genes in scRNA-seq data. These models are typically trained on large-scale, pooled datasets spanning 1 to 50 millions of cells from multiple studies. Public repositories such as Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO)\cite{edgar_gene_2002}, Human Cell Atlas\cite{regev_human_2017}, Human Ensemble Cell Atlas\cite{chen_heca_2022}, Deeply Integrated Human Single-Cell Omics Data (DISCO)\cite{li_disco_2022}, the European Molecular Biology Laboratory-European Bioinformatics Institute database (EMBL-EBI)\cite{papatheodorou_expression_2020},  \PanglaoDB{}\cite{franzen_panglaodb_2019}, and \cellxgene{}\cite{program_cz_2025} provide rich sources of harmonized single-cell data.

Several foundation models have been proposed, each introducing unique design and training strategies. \scBERT{}\cite{yang_scbert_2022}, a pioneering TFM based on the \BERT{} architecture\cite{devlin2018bert} and Performer attention\cite{choromanski2020rethinking}, employs masked language modeling (MLM) to predict masked gene expressions based on intra-cellular gene context. This model has demonstrated superior performance when compared to both traditional machine learning models and deep learning models that are not foundation-based, excelling in various downstream tasks such as cell type annotation. \Geneformer{}\cite{theodoris_transfer_2023} represents each cell as a sequence of ordered genes sorted by their median-normalized expression levels, and use MLM to predict masked genes based on other genes in the sequence. \UCE{}\cite{rosen_uce_2024} introduces a "masked or unexpressed" pretraining objective and leverages \esmTwo{}\cite{lin_evolutionary-scale_2023} protein embeddings to enable cross-species transfer.
%In this approach, each cell's gene expression profile is transformed into a sequence by sampling genes proportionally to their expression levels and arranging them according to their chromosomal positions. A subset of these genes is masked, and the remaining unmasked genes are input into a transformer model to generate a cell representation. The masked genes are then combined with randomly selected non-expressed genes from the same cell to create a set of query genes. Each gene is represented using protein embeddings derived from the \esmTwo{} model. The model is trained to predict the expression status of each query gene by leveraging these protein embeddings.
\scGPT{}\cite{cui_scgpt_2024}, an autoregressive model, adapts generative pretraining with specialized attention masking to handle the unordered nature of scRNA-seq data, enabling both gene and cell representation learning. \scFoundation{}\cite{hao_scfoundation_2024} adds read-depth-aware (RDA) pretraining task using hierarchical Bayesian downsampling to improve robustness across variable sequencing depths. 

More recently, \texttt{scCello}\cite{yuan_cell-ontology_2024-1} and \texttt{scMulan}\cite{ma_scmulan_2024} adopt a multi-task learning by combining self-supervised and supervised objectives. The self-supervised learning objective captures intrinsic relationships between genes within a cell, while the supervised objective leverages available metadata, such as cell type, organ type, tissue type, and external knowledge (e.g. cellular ontology relationships). This dual approach enabled the models to encode explicit relationships between cells, facilitating the capture of higher-level biological connections.

At the intersection of transcriptomics and nature language, models such as \texttt{GenePT}\cite{chen2024genept}, \texttt{scReader}\cite{li_screader_2024}, \texttt{cellWhisperer}\cite{schaefer_multimodal_2024} and \texttt{CELLama}\cite{choi_cellama_2024} explore joint training or prompting of large language models (LLMs) with transcriptomic representations. These methods aim to bridge the gap between natural language representations of genes and single-cell transcriptomics by utilizing both large language models (LLMs) and transcriptomic foundation models simultaneously.

In parallel, several studies have explored the impact of pretraining dataset size, cell-type diversity, and modeling strategies on pretraining effectiveness and model generalization\cite{boiarsky_deeper_2024,denadel_evaluating_2024,alsabbagh_foundation_2023}, as well as the zero-shot performance of TFMs on unseen cell types\cite{kedzierska_assessing_2023,hu_evaluation_2024}. However, research in this field is still in its early stages, and current findings suggest that TFMs may not yet be learning biologically meaningful representations, highlighting the need for further investigation and improved training strategies. While many prior models claim significant advances, their disparate implementations make it difficult to disentangle which innovations truly drive performance improvements.

In response to these challenges, we present \bmfmrna{}, a flexible and modular software package that supports, 
\begin{itemize}
    \item Configurable pretraining strategies, as introduced in existing TFMs,
    \item Whole-cell expression decoder (WCED), a novel pretraining objective, designed for the non-sequential nature of gene expression data and shown to outperform existing TFMs on zero-shot cell type clustering and annotation.
    \item Systematic evaluation on established benchmarks including cell type annotation, perturbation prediction, and batch integration, in both zero-shot and fine-tuning settings.
    \item Interpretability tools to analyze model predictions and gain insights into learned biological representations.
\end{itemize}

We show that with only 1\% of \cellxgene{} data, WCED-based models combined with multi-task learning, achieve performance on par with existing approaches in key downstream tasks such as cell type clustering and annotation. 
With 10\% of \cellxgene{} data, the WCED-trained models significantly outperform existing approaches on zero-shot cell-type clustering.
As we continue to expand \bmfmrna{} with support for multiomics integration and novel modeling strategies, we invite the research community to collaborate in advancing the development of robust and biologically meaningful TFMs.

The remainder of the paper is organized as follows: Section 2 introduces the \bmfmrna{} framework and datasets; Section 3 presents benchmark results across downstream tasks; Section 4 discuss our key findings; and Section 5 concludes with a summary and directions for future work.

\section{Materials and Methods}
\subsection{Datasets}
To construct a robust, generalizable foundation model for single-cell transcriptomics, we utilized a broad range of publicly available datasets that span tissues, organs, disease states, and sequencing protocols. These datasets are processed to LitData format to enable memory-efficient and parallel data streaming compatible with multi-GPU and distributed environments.
\subsubsection{Pretraining Datasets}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{\PanglaoDB{}\cite{franzen_panglaodb_2019}} is a manually curated repository that aggregates high-quality scRNA-seq data from public sources. It includes 1,368 samples, split into 305 human and 1,063 mouse runs. For this study, we only used the human samples for pretraining. After standardization and filtering, we retained over 1 million cells from 74 human tissues. These provide broad biological coverage and serve as a rich source of variation for pretraining.

\item \textbf{\cellxgene{}}\cite{program_cz_2025} provides a harmonized corpus of single-cell experiments, supporting reproducibility and structured metadata, and it consists of a suite of tools that help scientists to find, download, explore, analyze, annotate, and publish single-cell datasets.
The \cellxgene{} Discover Census offers efficient computational tools for accessing, querying, and analyzing the complete collection of scRNA-seq data available in \cellxgene{}. Using the TileDB-SOMA-backed Census, we curated a high-quality subset of data for model training and validation. The detailed data selection process is provided in the supplemental material. We used this preprocessed dataset for model pretraining.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Downstream Task Datasets}
We evaluated our models using the datasets from recently introduced \scEval{} benchmarking paper~\cite{liu_evaluating_2024}, which provides standardized datasets and evaluation protocols for a comprehensive range of single-cell analysis tasks. It includes datasets for \textit{cell type clustering}, assessing the ability to identify distinct cell populations, and \textit{batch integration}, which evaluates how well methods align data from different experiments while preserving biological variability. The suite also covers \textit{dropout recovery}, aimed at inferring gene expression values lost due to technical sparsity, and technical noise removal, focused on improving data quality by eliminating artifacts. Additional tasks include \textit{cell-type annotation} using known labels, \textit{gene function prediction} based on co-expression or network inference, \textit{perturbation prediction} to assess responses to experimental interventions, \textit{gene regulatory network reconstruction}, and \textit{simulation}, which tests methods on synthetic datasets with known ground truth. For more details on the benchmark datasets, we refer to \cite{liu_evaluating_2024}.


