\subsection{\bmfmrna{} Framework}

The \bmfmrna{} framework is composed of many modular components, as illustrated in Fig. \ref{fig:package-overview} and explained in detail here.

\subsubsection{Single-cell Dataloaders}
scRNA-seq data can be accessed from modern storage formats like TileDB-SOMA and the commonly used .h5ad (AnnData) files. TileDB-SOMA supports cloud-native, chunked access to massive datasets, making it ideal for scalable machine learning and interactive analysis, as seen in tools like \cellxgene{}. In contrast, .h5ad files are widely adopted for local workflows and integrate well with libraries such as ScanPy and anndata. To support pretraining of TFMs, we built a pipeline that converts both TileDB-SOMA and AnnData formats into LitData-compatible datasets. The LitData module from PyTorch Lightning enables efficient batching, transformation, and multi-GPU or distributed training, allowing us to scale model training to millions of cells. For fine-tuning, we rely on in-memory .h5ad files, as most fine-tuning tasks involve single datasets and do not require streaming or distributed infrastructure.

\subsubsection{Quality Control Module}
All datasets are processed using the Scanpy Python package, which provides efficient tools for filtering and normalizing large-scale scRNA-seq data. To ensure robust downstream performance, we apply a multi-stage quality control (QC) pipeline targeting both cells and genes.

At the cell level, we exclude low-quality entries based on several criteria. Cells with very low total UMI counts—often indicative of empty droplets or poor capture—are removed to avoid noise. We also discard cells with high mitochondrial content (above 15\% of total reads), as these are typically apoptotic or stressed. Similarly, cells with disproportionately high ribosomal RNA content are filtered out to reduce confounding effects from translational machinery. In some datasets, we also exclude rare or ambiguous cell types based on coarse ontology annotations, particularly when they are not well represented or may introduce instability during training.

At the gene level, we retain only those transcripts that are likely to contribute meaningful signal. Non-protein-coding genes are removed to focus on well-annotated functional transcripts. We also filter out genes with low dispersion—those that show minimal expression variance across the corpus—as well as genes that are expressed in only a very small fraction of cells, which often contribute more noise than signal in foundation model training.

Following filtering, we normalize the remaining gene expression matrix using log1p transformation to stabilize variance and compress the long-tailed distribution of expression counts. 

%Then, we perform median scaling to normalize each cell's total read depth relative to the dataset median, improving comparability across cells, batches, and tissues.

%This pipeline ensures that all inputs to the model are biologically relevant, technically sound, and standardized for cross-dataset modeling and pretraining.


\subsubsection{Data Selection Module}
% Recent work explored the role of pre-training dataset's size and diversity on the performance of single-cell foundation models and demonstrated
% current TFMs tend to plateau in performance with pre-training datasets that are only a fraction of the size. Inspired by this research, \bmfmrna{} allows users to choose from multiple strategies for selecting subsets of cells for pre-training. These include selecting all available cells, random sampling, priority-based round-robin sampling which ensures transcriptomic diversity while reducing dataset size. Additionally, \bmfmrna{} allows to choose a desired sampling percentage, enabling efficient model training on large single-cell datasets.

The size and composition of pre-training datasets play a critical role in shaping the capabilities of single-cell foundation models. Recent work\cite{denadel_evaluating_2024} has shown that scaling both model size and data diversity improves downstream performance, but with diminishing returns when adding highly redundant data. scRNA-seq datasets are typically large, imbalanced, and exhibit significant redundancy due to over-representation of commonly measured cell types (e.g., brain and blood cells) and high transcriptional similarity across many cells. Uniformly increasing dataset size without accounting for these factors can lead to computational inefficiency and limited performance gains.

To address these challenges, \bmfmrna{} provides an optional, configurable data selection module that supports multiple intelligent sampling strategies. This allows users to balance computational cost and biological diversity based on their pre-training objectives:

\begin{itemize}

\item \textbf{Uniform Sampling}: A simple baseline approach that performs random sub-sampling across the dataset. While fast, it may preserve existing class imbalances and under-sample rare or biologically informative cell states.

\item \textbf{Equal Downsample (Round-Robin Priority Sampling)}:\label{p:equal_downsample}
This strategy cycles through annotated strata (e.g., tissue, cell type, or condition) to ensure broad coverage of biological states. It is particularly useful when label balancing is desired, such as for multitask learning or zero-shot evaluation.

% \item \textbf{Geometric Sketching}: Inspired by manifold learning, this method selects a subset of cells that best spans the underlying transcriptional space. It prioritizes outliers and underrepresented populations by identifying points near the periphery or in sparse regions of the cell manifold, thereby increasing diversity and reducing redundancy.

\end{itemize}

In addition, \bmfmrna{} allows users to specify a desired sampling percentage, making it straightforward to pre-train models efficiently even on very large datasets. All sampling strategies are fully integrated into the data loader and can be easily toggled or customized. Users can apply them flexibly for large-scale pretraining, targeted fine-tuning on specific tissues, or robustness evaluation across rare cell populations.


\subsubsection{Cell-level Transformations Module}
% Cell-level transformations are essential for normalizing scRNA-seq data and ensuring that gene expression values are comparable across cells. These transformations, including log normalization, median normalization, gene expression ranking, random or chunk-based gene dropout, exclusion of non-expressed genes, and Bayesian/Poisson down-sampling, mitigate variations in sequencing depth, gene expression levels, and technical noise. This normalization enables more accurate downstream analysis and model training. Detailed descriptions of each transformation are provided in the supplemental material.

Cell-level transformations are critical for normalizing scRNA-seq data, mitigating technical variation, and enhancing model generalization. Beyond standard normalization (e.g., log transformation, median scaling), \bmfmrna{} incorporates a rich suite of cell-level transformations designed to align model behavior with the known properties of scRNA-seq data and to augment training with realistic biological and technical variations.

To simulate dropout events common in scRNA-seq, we implement both random dropout (mimicking stochastic gene loss due to capture inefficiency) and ordered dropout (removing top-expressed genes deterministically, enabling robustness studies and curriculum learning). To model sequencing depth variation explicitly, we adopt a dead-depth-aware Bayesian down-sampling strategy, inspired by \scFoundation{}~\cite{hao_scfoundation_2024}. Cells are probabilistically down-sampled from a target read depth to a sampled read depth, while the model is trained to reconstruct original expression profiles conditioned on read depth, promoting robustness to variable sequencing coverage.

TFMs often rely on an explicit gene ordering. We support configurable gene sorting strategies, including expression-based sorting (ordering genes by expression level, used in autoregressive or masked training) and metadata-based sorting (ordering by genomic or regulatory properties). For models that treat gene expression as discrete tokens, we provide expression binning methods such as quantile binning (equal-coverage bins) and logarithmic binning (log-spaced bins capturing fold changes).

These transformations collectively enhance model robustness, enable simulation of biologically and technically plausible variations, and promote generalization across diverse datasets and modalities. Full details of each transformation are provided in the Supplementary Material.

\subsubsection{Multitask Input-Output Preparation Module}
Our framework employs a flexible multitask learning setup where each input sample, comprised of gene identifiers, expression values, and optional metadata, is processed by a shared encoder and routed to one or more downstream task-specific heads. The input preparation is handled by tokenizers, collators, and maskers, which support variable input configurations and seamlessly align with the model’s objectives.
We describe the software component here, the mathematical description of how the fields are combined can be found in Sec. \ref{s:input-representation} below.

\paragraph{Multifield Tokenizers and Collators} 
Transcriptomic samples are described by genes and their measured levels in the sample. 
\bmfmrna{}, following the ideas developed in \scBERT, \scGPT and others inputs this model by producing embeddings for each gene token and it corresponding expression level, and by summing them. 
In this context, genes and expressions are treated as ``fields'' and their combined input as a multifield input.


\bmfmrna{} handles this by wrapping standard Huggingface tokenizers for each field in a MultifieldTokenizer object, which ensures multiple fields are aligned correctly, including potential continuous values for some fields.  The collator controls batch preparation, including the tokenization, handling operations like cell-level transforms, masking, and zero-expression padding as needed. This ensures the model receives well-structured, properly formatted multi-field batches for effective training. This approach is especially useful for TFMs as they involving multiple aligned fields as inputs (eg. genes, expressions, state vector such as perturbation state) and labels (eg. for masked genes, expressions, or post-perturbation expression).

\paragraph{Training Objectives}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Masked Expression Prediction}: In this training objective, a certain fraction of the expression values are replaced with MASK tokens and their values predicted.
This is the most widely used training objective for TFMs, implemented in \scBERT{}~\cite{yang_scbert_2022}, \scGPT{}~\cite{cui_scgpt_2024}, \scFoundation{}~\cite{hao_scfoundation_2024} and others.
As in \scFoundation, \bmfmrna{} supports masked expression prediction where the input is downsampled (RDA method).

\bmfmrna{} supports this task in several ways, which are configurable by the user.
Expression values can be tokenized, and then predicted using standard cross entropy loss. 
Prior to tokenization, expression values are typically binned using a percentile method, or following log-normalization.
Alternatively, the values can be predicted as floating points without binning, using a MSE loss (assuming the values have been log-normalized).
Gene expression in single cell data is a zero-inflated phenomenon with a large number of zeros, representing absence of detection in the experiment.
To treat during training, \bmfmrna{} predicts two tasks for each expression value, a binary task predicting whether the gene had expression at all, and a continuous valued task predicting the expression level.

In between these two approaches, \bmfmrna{} also supports a new loss called ``token value loss'' in which the logits used for predicting the token are multiplied by the numerical values of the tokens and compared with the numerical value of the expression level. A more detailed analysis of this approach can be found in Zausinger et al.~\cite{zausinger2025regressdontguess}.

\item \textbf{Masked Gene Prediction}: Masked gene prediction is the task that most closely follows masked language modeling from the world of NLP.
Genes in a sequence are replaced with a MASK token with a certain probability and the model is tasked with predicting which gene was masked.
\bmfmrna{} supports the standard version of gene masking as employed in \Geneformer{}~\cite{theodoris_transfer_2023}, and also adds the ability to perform gene masking in sequences where expression values are explicitly included in the input. 
Gene and expression masking can be used simultaneously during training.

