\item \textbf{Whole-cell Expression Decoder (WCED)}\label{p:wced}:
Using the \bmfmrna{} framework, we implemented a new pretraining objective that is centered around predicting the expression levels for the whole cell at once, rather than limiting to just the masked genes.
The training objective is designed to force the model to learn cell representations, and therefore the decoder uses the CLS token output of the encoder only as input for the decoder, creating an autoencoder-like bottleneck.
The WCED objective takes any input format for the cell, and produces two logits vectors in the shape of the gene vocab, one to calculate the binary expressed/not-expressed loss, and one to calculate the MSE loss.
One of the critical questions for training with WCED is how much of the cell's input to provide to the model.
As the input decreases, the non-input prediction (masking) becomes harder, but the input prediction (autoencoder) becomes easier.
Our goal is for the model to learn to map any length of cell input to the same representation, which can reconstruct the full cell expression.

This training objective is similar but distinct relative to several others that have been discussed in recent publications. \scPrint{}~\cite{kalfon_scprint_2024} used a bottleneck task but without predicting masked values, UCE~\cite{rosen_uce_2024} used the CLS representation to predict whether non-input genes were expressed or not, and \scGPT{}~\cite{cui_scgpt_2024} included the GEPC task to predict all of the masked expression values from the CLS representation.
WECD differs from these in that it pushes the model to map diverse partial inputs to the same CLS representation.
We find that this approach provides superior results as described below.

\item \textbf{Cell-level metadata prediction}: \bmfmrna{} enables the user to supply a list of losses which are either defined at the token level (eg, masked gene prediction, expression value prediction, binary expression prediction) or at the sequence level.
Sequence level tasks include predicting cell type, tissue type, disease state, or other categorical or regression tasks.
Categorical tasks can use cross entropy or focal loss~\cite{lin_focal_loss_2020}.
Certain labels can also be requested by the user to include a gradient reversal layer~\cite{ganingrl2015}, between the encoder and the classifier to correct for batch effects.

These tasks can be performed simultaneously with the language modeling as part of multitask learning, or on their own.

\end{itemize}
This multitask design enables unified training on diverse datasets while supporting strong task-specific performance. The custom tokenizers and maskers abstract away the complexity of input encoding, allowing users to scale to multiple datasets and objectives without modifying preprocessing pipelines.

\subsubsection{Input Representation}\label{s:input-representation}
The input to our model is designed to flexibly represent both gene identity and gene expression level, with the option of incorporating various levels of biological prior knowledge via pretrained embeddings. The encoder processes each cell as a structured sequence of gene-expression pairs, with embeddings constructed from multiple token types. This design supports both expressive modeling and downstream interpretability.

\paragraph{Mathematical Formulation}
scRNA-seq data provides a high-resolution view of gene expression at the single-cell level processed into cell-gene matrix.  Formally, let $M \in R_{NxG}$ be the cell-gene matrix, where $N\in {1, 2 ....,n}$ represent the number of cells, $G\in {1, 2 ....,g}$ represent the genes, then $M_{ng}$ represents the read count or expression of the $g^{th}$ gene in $n^{th}$ cell. The basic input to the \bmfmrna{} model is at a single cell consisting (1) genes in the cell, (2) gene expression profile, (3) gene-level states to represent metadata information associated with individual genes such as perturbation experiment states and (4) cell-level states to represent metadata information associated with individual cells such as cell type, tissue and disease contexts that are captured in large-scale single-cell transcriptomic datasets.

\paragraph{Gene Encoding}
Beyond the textual representations discussed above, genes can also be specifically encoded to reflect the diversity of biological scales and phenomena they are involved in. Genes can be represented by their gene names as labels, together with their respective level of expression in the cell. But we may also want to include external knowledge by injecting representations of the RNA, DNA or protein sequences that can vary in length depending on the nucleotides or amino acids considered, epigenomic marks consisting of DNA methylation state or histone acetylation states and finally by their regulatory networks.
Accordingly, the \bmfmrna{} framework supports multiple gene representations adapted from previously introduced FMs. Specially, it supports random initialization of genes as well as initialization from embeddings obtained using previously pre-trained models that capture distributed representation of genes such as gene2vec using gene co-expression\cite{du2019gene2vec}, representations of protein structures from \esmTwo{}\cite{lin2023evolutionary}, DNA representations from \DNABERTTwo{}\cite{zhou2023dnabert} and Borzoi\cite{linder2025predicting} for DNA and epigenomic representations. 
\bmfmrna is unique among TFMs in that it supports partial coverage by external embeddings, so that the model can learn random embeddings for non-protein coding genes alongside pretrained embeddings based on protein embeddings, or other variations.
\bmfmrna supports expression encoding as well, though, as shown by \Geneformer and by UCE, it is possible to encode expression level information indirectly via the order of the genes or the number of repetitions, respectively. 
%  In this respect \Geneformer{} is not that different from \scBERT{}\cite{yang_scbert_2022} which represented each gene-expression pair in the cell sequence via the sum of a learnable expression embedding and gene embedding. 
% \Geneformer{} differs in not allowing ties in expression levels and more significantly, by masking the gene token, not the expression token.

\paragraph{Expression Encoding}\label{p:cve}
In addition to the simple binning and tokenization approach used by \scBERT and some versions of \scGPT, \bmfmrna also supports continuous value encoding so that the full numerical information is available to the model in a learnable way. This is supported via two approaches:

\textit{Multi-layered Perceptron}: The MLP-based encoder for continuous features transforms each input expression value \( x_i \) through a series of fully connected layers with non-linear activations. This process can be represented as:

\[
f_i(x_i) = W_n \cdot \phi(W_{n-1} \cdot \phi(\cdots \phi(W_1 x_i + b_1) \cdots) + b_{n-1}) + b_n
\]

Where \( W_i \) are the weight matrices, \( b_i \) are the biases, and \( \phi(\cdot) \) is the activation function, such as ReLU or GELU. This allows the model to learn complex, non-linear relationships.

\textit{Periodic activation}: The periodic activation-based encoder, uses sine and cosine functions to capture these patterns. For a expression value \( x_i \), the transformation is:

\[
v_i = 2\pi c_i x_i \quad \text{and} \quad f_i(x_i) = \text{concat}[\sin(v_i), \cos(v_i)]
\]

Where \( c_i \) is a trainable frequency parameter, and the sine and cosine functions map the feature into a periodic embedding. After this transformation, a linear projection is applied:

\[
f_{i, \text{final}} = W_2 \cdot \phi(W_1 f_i(x_i) + b_1) + b_2
\]

Additionally, the zero-special token mechanism is used to distinguish zero (or negative) values from other features. When enabled, a zero in the input is treated as a special token and mapped to an index in an embedding layer. This can be represented as:

\[
\text{special\_token}(x_i) = \begin{cases} 
\text{idx}_{\text{special}} & \text{if } x_i = 0 \\
x_i & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

The special token is then passed through an embedding layer, \( E_{\text{special}} \), to get its corresponding embedding:

\[
\text{special\_embedding}(x_i) = E_{\text{special}}(\text{idx}_{\text{special}})
\]

Where \( \text{idx}_{\text{special}} \) is the index for the zero-special token. This mechanism ensures that zeros are treated distinctly from regular features, which is crucial to represent genes that not expressed in a cell.



\subsubsection{Models, Training, Experiment tracking and Intepretability}


We build on top of the Hugging Face Transformers framework to implement our customized models, wrapping their tokenizer and model APIs to support multi-field inputs, including gene identity, expression values, perturbation states, and optional metadata. All models operate on variable-length sequences and employ multi-head output layers for multitask learning.

Model training is orchestrated with PyTorch Lightning, enabling distributed multi-GPU training and mixed-precision execution. Hydra is used to manage experiment configurations in a modular and reproducible manner, allowing users to define and override hyperparameters, data paths, model types, and training strategies via YAML. We use TorchMetrics to compute a comprehensive set of performance metrics.

Our training objectives are tailored to the multi-task nature of our models. We use cross-entropy loss for categorical classification (e.g., cell type or tissue prediction), mean squared error (MSE) and zero-inflated MSE for modeling continuous gene expression values, focal loss for class imbalance, binary cross-entropy for multi-label tasks, and gradient reversal layers to enable domain-adversarial training for batch correction and dataset adaptation.

Experiments are tracked with ClearML, which records hyperparameters, system resource usage, model checkpoints, and evaluation metrics. This provides full reproducibility and enables real-time visualization and comparison of experimental runs.

For interpretability, we integrate Captum~\cite{kokhlikyan2020captum} and employ the integrated gradients technique ~\cite{sundararajan_integrated_gradients_2017} to compute gene-level attributions. This technique provides a principled method to quantify the contribution of each input feature—such as individual genes or expression values—to the model’s output by integrating gradients along a path from a baseline to the actual input. In our setting, Integrated Gradients enables us to assess how much each gene or input token influences a given prediction, whether for cell type classification, expression reconstruction, perturbation response prediction, or masked token recovery. Such interpretability is critical for biological validation, offering insights into which genes drive model behavior and helping to detect spurious correlations or biologically implausible patterns.

Together, this modeling stack, Hugging Face Transformers, PyTorch Lightning, TorchMetrics, Hydra, ClearML, and Captum, provides a powerful, flexible, and reproducible framework for experimenting with complex multi-field input models and deriving biologically grounded insights.

