\subsection{Pretraining Experiments}
The \bmfmrna{} framework supports a wide range of pretraining objectives and strategies. In this section, we present the results of three pretrained models built using the framework, selected to explore complementary hypotheses about effective pretraining for TFMs. The models differ in their masking strategies, auxiliary objectives, input length, and training schedule, allowing us to assess the trade-offs between these design choices.
All models were pretrained on the 1\% equal downsample (see Section \ref{p:equal_downsample} above) of the \cellxgene{} corpus (2025-01-30 release), consisting of 284,501 samples drawn from the original 28 million sample set. To create a balanced subset, we stratified by the combination of cell type, tissue, tissue general, and disease columns, resulting in 4,718 groups downsampled to 53 samples each, and 2,246 smaller groups where all available samples were included. The four models compared are:
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{MLM+RDA}: trained with expression masking and RDA downsampling, following the settings in scFoundation.

\item \textbf{MLM+Multitask}: trained with both masked gene and masked expression objectives, along with multi-label classification (cell type, tissue, tissue general), and an adversarial loss to unlearn donor ID.

\item \textbf{WCED+Multitask}: trained first using WCED with random gene order and log-normalization, then fine-tuned with multitask objectives similar to the MLM+Multitask model.
\item \textbf{WCED 10 pct}: trained using WCED with random gene order and log-normalization.
\end{itemize}

Note that the models also differ in input length (4,096 vs. 1,024), masking rates (30\% vs. 15\%), and training schedules (20 epochs vs. warmup + 20 epochs vs. 20 + 20 epochs), which we explicitly retain to assess the impact of each pretraining strategy. While this introduces variability in total training time, it reflects realistic trade-offs in model design and optimization.

\subsubsection{MLM+RDA}
This model was trained using the MLM objective where the input has been downsampled using the RDA algorithm, with the same settings as in \scFoundation{}~\cite{hao_scfoundation_2024}, using the implementation of \bmfmrna{}.
The model is a standard \BERT{} architecture with hidden size 768, 12 attention heads and 12 layers, with positional encodings removed.
Expression counts are represented by encoding the continuous values with the periodic activation described in Sec.\ref{p:cve}, with 48 trainable basis dimensions, then summed with the gene embedding vectors.
For the masked expression levels, two losses are calculated, a binary "is zero" task and a continuous valued mean squared error loss term. 
If the ground truth value for the gene is zero, the MSE loss term is not included in backpropagation.
Training was done for 20 epochs on the 1\% downsampled \cellxgene{} dataset with 30\% expression masking with 4,096 input length. 
For samples with more than 4,096 non-zero genes, no zeros are input or predicted by the model. 
If there are fewer than 4,096 non-zero genes, zeros are appended after the non-zero values, with priority to zeros for genes that had non-zero expression in other samples in the same batch.
Of the 30\% of the tokens that the loss is calculated over, 5\% are input to the model unmasked, as a value calibration task. 

\subsubsection{MLM+Multitask}
This model was trained using a combination of two MLM objectives, expression value and masked gene prediction, and three label prediction objectives, cell type prediction, tissue prediction and tissue\_general prediction.
The model was warmed up by training for one epoch on the 10\% \cellxgene{} expression MLM only task, then trained for 20 epochs on the 1\% downsampled dataset.
The model is a standard \BERT{} architecture with hidden size 768, 12 attention heads and 12 layers, with positional encodings removed.
The information about expression levels was encoded using the MLP encoder described in section \ref{p:cve}, with expression level representation vectors and gene embedding vectors summed before input to the model.
The training loss is a sum of several loss terms.
For language modeling, the sequences have masked genes and masked expressions, both at rates of 15\%. Genes are predicted with cross entropy loss and expression levels with the combined "is zero" and MSE losses, as in the RDA only model.
Additionally, the model is trained with three sequence classification cross entropy loss terms for \cellxgene{}'s cell type, tissue and tissue\_general types, plus a sequence classification term with a gradient reversal layer~\cite{ganingrl2015} for unlearning donor id from the representation.

\subsubsection{WCED+Multitask}
This model was trained first with 20 epochs of WCED with input size of 1,024, on the 1\% equal downsample \cellxgene{} corpus, and then for an additional 20 epochs on the same data with multitask learning, where the WCED task was continued, along with predicting cell type, tissue type, general tissue type and donor id with a reverse gradient layer.

Samples were prepared with genes with randomized order, log-normalized and then truncated after 1,024 genes. The training loss was equally weighted between the autoencoder task (predicting the input genes), and the masking task (predicting the noninput genes), with each contributing a term for binary cross entropy loss and mean squared error loss.

\subsubsection{WCED 10\%}
We trained an additional model with the same size and methods as the WCED+Multitask model with WCED only on a 10 pct equal downsample training corpus. 
This corpus uses the same dataset split and downsampling logic as the 1\% equal downsample set but with a downsampling rate of 10\% instead of 1\%.
The corpus contains 2,818,963 samples with 1,966 groups defined by columns 'cell\_type', 'tissue', 'tissue\_general', 'disease' of size 975 each, and 4,998 groups from which all samples were taken. 
Training was executed for 7 epochs of this larger corpus using the WCED objective with MLP continuous value encoding.

%, but unlike the WCED+Multitask checkpoint, this model did not continue training afterward with a multitask objective.

