\subsubsection{Gene Perturbation}
We measure our expression level vector prediction using Pearson correlation, with various metrics, similarly to what has been done previously in scGPT \cite{}. 
    For a single perturbed cell, we denote by $v_g$ the vector of the ground truth values of expression levels after perturbation. We denote by $v_p$ the vector of our predicted values of expression level. In addition, we denote by $\overline{c}$ the vector of the mean expression values for all control cells.
    Since most genes do not change in expression levels between before and after the perturbation, we include also metrics that measure our performance on the top 20 differentially expressed genes between all control cells and all perturbed cells. Our metrics this include:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\rho$: Pearson correlation between our predicted vector $v_p$ and ground truth vector $v_g$.
        \item $\rho^{top20}$: Pearson correlation between our predicted vector $v_p$ and ground truth vector $v_g$, for the top 20 differentially expressed genes.
        \item $\rho_{delta}$: Pearson correlation between our predicted vector minus the mean over controls $v_p - \overline{c}$, and the ground truth vector minus the mean over controls $v_g - \overline{c}$.
        \item $\rho_{delta}^{top20}$: Pearson correlation between our predicted vector minus the mean over controls $v_p - \overline{c}$, and the ground truth vector minus the mean over controls $v_g - \overline{c}$, for the top 20 differentially expressed genes.
    \end{itemize}
Table~\ref{tab:pert_res} shows our results on test perturbations (in which non of the perturbed genes was seen in the training data) compared to those of scGPT \cite{}, demonstrating that our model attains comparable results to SOTA.


