\paragraph{Gene Perturbation}
Perturbation experiments allow us to measure and understand the cell response to genetic manipulation such as knocking out a gene, or changing its expression level by experimental manipulations. In such experiments, a population of cells undergoes a perturbation in a particular gene or pairs of genes, and the RNA expression level of all genes is then measured. In addition, a seperate population of cells is left as a control population, that does not undergo the perturbation, and its expression levels are measured as well. Importantly, there is no way to measure a before-after expression level profile of a single cell since the measurement itself is destructive. We are thus left with a population of control cells, and a population of perturbed cells. with almost 20000 protein coding genes, and the possible combinations of genes to be perturbed simultaneously, it is unreasonable to be able to experimentally measure the expression profile of every perturbation. Using pre-trained models to predict the outcome of a perturbation is thus a very useful alternative. 
We use our model to learn patterns of expression levels after gene perturbation using various datasets. We focus on two main datasets: the Adamson dataset \cite{} - a leukemia cell line pertrubation dataset consisting of 87 single gene perturbations, and Norman dataset \cite{} - a leukemia cell line pertrubation dataset consisting of 131 gene pairs perturbations and 105 single gene perturbations.

During training, we randomly select a control cell from the entire set of control cells, and pair it to a perturbed cell. This allows to create a 'before-after' picture, which we expect to help the model learn how expressions levels vary in control cells and how they change in perturbed cells. Since the model gets to see all the perturbed cells many times, with different control cells paired to it, we expect this to reduce noise from natural variation and highlight the actual changes due to the perturbation itself. 
