\section{ Continuous Set of Prices }

 Now we extend these ideas to a more generic price set $\cup_{\delta: [0,\infty)]} P_\delta$. We want to investigate the equilibrium of the game $\G(q)$ in such generic case. We begin with analysis of the game $\G(q)$ before the partial choking point $\bqd$.


\subsection{Solution of  $\G(q)$ before partial choking point:}
It is important to observe that because of the nature of utility function (see \eqref{eqn_util_cases})
, the game among the manufacturers exhibits \textbf{discontinuities}. This discontinuity arises from the structure of our demand model comprising of loyal- strategic customers.
The discontinuous nature of the game  poses unique challenges in equilibrium analysis. Unlike traditional continuous games where standard equilibrium concepts apply, here, manufacturers must account for abrupt shifts in demand dynamics. The lack of Nash equilibrium suggests that \textbf{price competition leads to oscillatory or unstable behaviors}, reinforcing the real-world observations of price fluctuations in  marketplaces.

In such a setting, one can attempt to look for mixed strategy Nash equilibrium. However, in practice, it is observed that the  manufacturers tend to adopt \textbf{pure strategies} rather than mixed strategies. The key reason is that implementing a mixed strategy requires playing the game repeatedly without any guarantee of a stable outcome, which is often unrealistic in practical scenarios.
As a result, we observe a natural deviation from mixed strategies, leading us to explore alternative equilibrium concepts.(see \cite{Walunj,Piliouras}).

Given the above observations, we adopt the recently introduced notion of an \textit{equilibrium cycle}, as described in \cite{Walunj}. This concept captures the dynamic nature of manufacturer strategies in response to fluctuating demand and competition.
The introduction of equilibrium cycles provides a more realistic description of the strategic behavior observed in highly competitive markets, such as the price variations seen in Figure \ref{fig_Amazon}.








