\subsection{ Supplier's Problem }
The supplier as the leader of the Stackelberg game would already anticipate the unrest in the downstream market because of the equilibrium cycle among the manufacturers. Now the primary question for the supplier is that what is the optimal price $q^{*}$  which the supplier quotes to the downstream manufacturers. Another important question is that would  $q^{*}\le \bqd $ or would $\bqd < q^{*} 
\le \bqm$ that is would it prefer to choke one manufacturer or would prefer that  manufacturers remain in duopoly?. The main question is that in order to find the optimal price quoted by the supplier, how should the utility of the supplier be defined. Will it just consider the revenue or would also consider the variations in the demand because of the unrest in the downstream market.  In this paper, we consider the following metrics for capturing the utility of the supplier:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item [i)] Maximum Revenue generated 
    \item[ii)] Minimum Revenue generated
    \item[iii)] Variability in demand
\end{enumerate}
\begin{thm}\label{thm_shrink_eq_1}
For $q \le \bqd$, the length of the equilibrium cycle decreases with increase in $q$. 
$$
\nicefrac{|d\pdl- d\pdu|}{dq} < 0.
$$
\end{thm}


