 Each manufacturer changes it's price at alternating time steps, the manufacturer that doesn't change it's price at a particular time step quotes the same price as previous time step.\\
 \noindent \textbf{ Actions } : The action at each decision epoch is for the manufacturer to choose the price among the discrete set $P_\delta = [0,\delta \cdots ]$ if the opponent manufacturer has changed it's price at the previous time step, else it keeps it's price same as previous time step. That is,
 \begin{eqnarray*}
     \A_i^{t}(x) = 
     \begin{cases}
         p_i \mbox{ if } t \in \{ 2,4 \cdots \} \\
         P_\delta \mbox{ else }
     \end{cases}
 \end{eqnarray*}
 At each time \( t \), only one manufacturer updates its price:
\begin{itemize}
    \item If \( t \in T_i \), manufacturer \( M_i \) selects a new price \( a_i \in \mathcal{A}_i(x_t) \), while \( M_j \) retains its previous price.
    \item If \( t \in T_j \), manufacturer \( M_j \) selects a new price \( a_j \in \mathcal{A}_j(x_t) \), while \( M_i \) retains its previous price.
\end{itemize}

 \noindent \textbf{ Policies  }: Define Stationary Markov Deterministic policy $\pi = \{ \pi(x) \forall x \in X (t) \}$.  

 In particular we consider the following policy, which is important for us, in the sense it becomes a part of the NE, 
 Now given a state $x = (p_i,p_j)$ define the following:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
  \pi_i^{*}  = 
  \begin{cases}
      p_i  &\mbox{ if } t \in \{ 2,4 \cdots \} \\
      p_{j} - \delta & \mbox{ else if }  t \in \{ 1,3 \cdots \}  \mbox{ and } p_{j} \in (\pdwn_d, \pup_d] \\
      \pup_d  &\mbox{ if } t \in \{ 1,3 \cdots \} \mbox{ and } p_j \le \pdwn_d \\
  \end{cases}  
 \end{eqnarray*}
 We can similarly define $\pi_j^{*}$ as above.\\


 \noindent \textbf{ Utility }: The appropriate instantaneous utility of agent $M_i$ at time $t$ when the state is $x \in X_t$ and action $a_i \in \A_{i}^{t}(x)$ and the opponent chooses the policy $\pi_j^{*}$ is given by:
 \begin{eqnarray}\label{util_mdp_manu_i}
   \overline{U_i}(x, a_i, \pi_j^{*}) = E \left(\sum_{t\in \{1,3\cdots\}} \beta^t U_i(a_i|x,\pi_j^{*})\right),
 \end{eqnarray}
 where $\beta \in [0,1)$ denotes the disconting factor.\\
 \noindent \textbf{ Transition Probabilities } : The controlled transition probabilities of the markov chain  given a state $x = (p_i,p_j)$ is as follows :
 \begin{eqnarray*}
p_i^{t}(x'|x,a_i, \pi_j^{*}) =  
\begin{cases}
    1 \mbox{ if } x^{'} = (p_i, \pi^*_j(p_i)) \mbox{ and } t = \{2,4\cdots\},\\
    1 \mbox{ if }x^{'} = (\pup_d, p_j) \mbox{ and } p_j \le \pdwn_d \mbox{ and } t = \{1,3.\cdots\}\\
    1 \mbox{ if }, x^{'} = ( p_j- \delta, p_j) \mbox{ and } p_j \in (\pdwn_d ,\pup_d]  \mbox{ and } t = \{1,3.\cdots\} \mbox{ and } \\
    0 \mbox{ else }
\end{cases}
 \end{eqnarray*}.\\
 \noindent \textbf{ Controlled Dynamics } :
Let the state of the system at time \( t \) be \( x_t = (p_{i,t}, p_{j,t}) \). The system transitions as follows:
\begin{align*}
    \text{At } t \in T_i: \quad & p_{i,t} = a_i, \quad p_{j,t} = p_{j,t-1} \\
    \text{At } t+1 \in T_j: \quad & p_{j,t+1} = \pi_j^*(p_{i,t}), \quad p_{i,t+1} = p_{i,t}
\end{align*}

Hence, the system evolves deterministically over two steps, influenced by the actions of both manufacturers:
\[
x_t \xrightarrow{a_i} (a_i, p_{j,t}) \xrightarrow{\pi_j^*} (a_i, \pi_j^*(a_i)) = x_{t+2}
\]

\noindent \textbf{ Two-Step Bellman Update } : 
Since \( M_i \) acts only at odd time steps, its value function updates every two steps. The Bellman equation is given by:
\[
