\subsubsection*{Saddle Node Bifurcation}
Let the governing dynamics of the salinity difference be denoted by $f$ such that 
\[f(y, \theta) := p^* -y[ 1 + m^2(y-\theta)^2] \] 

We can derive a condition for which a saddle node bifurcation exists. Suppose that $y^*$ is a stable equilibrium for fixed parameter $\theta$ and that $\theta_c$ is a critical parameter value. Locally, the governing dynamics are approximated with a Taylor expansion about $(y^*, \theta_c)$.
\begin{align}
    f(y,\theta) &\approx T(y^*,\theta_c) \notag \\
                &=  f(y^*, \theta_c) + f_y(y_0, \theta_c)(y-y^*) + f_\theta (y^*,\theta_c)(\theta - \theta_c) \\
                & \ \ \ + \frac{1}{2}f_{yy}(y_c,\theta_c)(y-y^*)^2 + \mathcal{O}(\theta^2) \notag
\end{align}

To get the normal form of the saddle node bifurcation, $f(y,\theta) = \theta + y^2$, the following conditions must hold. First the equilibrium condition must hold, that $f_y(y^*, \theta_c) = 0 $. This condition is met because $f_y(y^*, \theta_c) = 0 $ since $y^*$ is an equilibrium point. Moreover, the linear degeneracy condition must hold as well, that $f_y(y^*, \theta_c) = 0$. This condition holds so long as the following equation is satisfied. 

\[ 4m^2 y^* \theta_c - m^2\theta_c^2 - 2m^2 (y^*)^2-(y^*)^2 -1 =0. \]
The critical parameter curve is implicitly defined by the oddly symmetric hyperbolic equation.

