\section{Does structural complexity pay off?}\label{complexity}

Table~\ref{tab:dic_comparison} reports the Deviance Information Criterion  for the eight model specifications considered. Two clear patterns emerge. First, the DIC strongly favors models with stochastic volatility, particularly those with volatility in the idiosyncratic errors. Second, the role of dynamic heterogeneity is less clear-cut: models with heterogeneous dynamics are preferred only when volatility is absent from the idiosyncratic component. These results suggest that allowing for time-varying volatility plays a more dominant role in improving in-sample fit than adding lagged factor loadings.

