\subsection*{Effective Mode Volume and Enhanced Emission from Quantum  Emitters}\label{sec:applications}
For application of PCWs in nonlinear quantum optics,
two key design metrics are effective mode volume 
(per unit cell) and Purcell enhancement of a single quantum emitter (or dipole). The effective mode volume is a measure of the distribution of the mode withing the PCW (or a measure of the local field strength more generally), and governs the strength of the light-matter interaction, with a smaller mode volume leading to stronger light-matter interactions \cite{MangaRao2007}. As the field maximum, assuming the dipole is aligned in polarization with the mode, then 
\begin{equation}
    V_{\text{eff}}=\frac{\int_{\text{cell}}d\bm r\epsilon(\bm r)|{\bm e}_k(\bm r)|^2}{{\rm max}[\epsilon(\bm r)|
    {\bm e}_k(\bm r)|^2]},
\end{equation}
though, more generally, it depends on
both the polarization of the emitter and the position of the emitter \cite{PhysRevA.106.033514}, which we use below.


% Typically, in literature, the effective mode volume is defined as $V_{\text{eff}}=\text{min}\left(V_{\text{eff}}(\bm r)\right)$, but the position dependence is important for calculating the Purcell enhancement of embedded quantum emitters \cite{PhysRevA.106.033514}. 

The Purcell factor is a measure 
of the dipole enhanced spontaneous emission rate in the medium,
relative to a homogeneous medium~\cite{MangaRao2007}. For embedded 
quantum dipole emitters (such as quantum dots), treated as point dipoles,  it is defined as 
\begin{equation}
    \text{PF}({\bm r}_0)=\frac{3\pi c^2 an_g|\bm e_k({\bm r}_0)\cdot \hat{\bm n}|^2}{\omega_d^2\sqrt{\epsilon}}
\end{equation}
where the quantum emitter
(at position ${\bm r}_0$)
is assumed to be in the slab medium (with dielectric constant $\epsilon_b$),
has a dipole in the $\hat{\bm n}$ direction, and an emission wavelength of $\omega_d$. If the quantum emitter dipole aligns with the electric field, the Purcell factor can be written as
\begin{equation}
    \text{PF}({\bm r}_{0})=\frac{3\pi c^2an_g}{V_{\text{eff}} \omega_d^2\epsilon^{3/2}},
\end{equation}
and we see explicitly that the enhancement depends on the group index as well as the effective mode volume. 

Looking at how the minimum mode volume was affected during the optimization process, we find that for the W1 waveguide, the mode volume changes from $0.28\  [(\lambda/n)^3]$ to $0.34\  [(\lambda/n)^3]$, where $\lambda$ 
%\sh{these numbers look too big, but you need to use $\lambda_0/n$, is that waht you are using?}
is the wavelength of the $\tilde k=0.33$ point and $n=3.46$ is the index of refraction of the slab, or  $0.007$ [$\mu \text{m}^3]$ to $0.0086\ [\mu \text{m}^3]$. While for the ZIW waveguide, the mode volume went from 
%\sh{These are way too large - check units - should be less than 1 } \nir{Not sure how small this is. It's $10\%$ and $20\%$ as far as I can tell, which would lead to a corresponding change to the Purcell factor}
$0.23\  [(\lambda/n)^3]$ to $0.15\  [(\lambda/n)^3]$, or $0.0054$ [$\mu \text{m}^3]$ to $0.0036\ [\mu \text{m}^3]$. Additionally, there is a large electric field within one of the air holes of the ZIW waveguide design as can be seen in Fig.~\ref{fig:results}(d), which, for example, can be used for applications in all optical trapping. The maximum Purcell enhancement within the W1 waveguide is originally $1.32$ and changes to $1.17$ while the ZIW waveguide starts with a Purcell enhancement of $1.60$ which decreases to $1.67$. 

Although we find that the mode volume and Purcell enhancement for the W1 waveguide design is slightly worsened through the optimization process, we find a relatively equivariant improvement in the mode volume and Purcell enhancement for the ZIW waveguide. Since we find alternate effects from the two waveguide designs, it appears that decreasing backscattering loss does not have an inherent impact on the mode volume or Purcell enhancement. Finally, the mode structure of each of the final designs is no longer symmetric, so to couple into the waveguide, a specialized coupler may be needed.

