\section{Introduction}
Let $q$ be a prime power, $\fq$ be the finite field of order $q$, and $\fq^*$ be the multiplicative group of $\fq$. The factorization of polynomials over $\fq$ is a classical problem in finite field theory, with significant applications in areas such as coding theory~\cite{[Ber68]} and cryptography~\cite{[Len91]}. For a positive integer $n$, consider the polynomial $f_n(X)= X^n - 1 \in \fq[X]$. A notable property is that each irreducible factor of $f_n(X)$ over $\fq$ corresponds to a cyclic code of length $n$ over $\fq$ (see~\cite{[Van98]}). Consequently, determining all irreducible factors of $f_n(X)$ over $\fq$ is of paramount importance.

Over the past few decades, the irreducible factorization of the binomial $f_n(X)$ has been a challenging problem. Several authors have investigated the factorization of $f_n(X)$ under specific conditions, as explored, for example, in ~\cite{[BGM93],[CLT13],[Mey96],[WW12]}, with more general results obtained in~\cite{[BRS19],[Gra23]}. One of the most significant results is presented in~\cite{[BMOV15]}, which provides an explicit factorization of $f_n(X)$ over $\fq$ under the condition that each prime factor of $n$ divides $q^2 - 1$. 

Under certain conditions, an intriguing phenomenon emerges: each irreducible factor of $f_n(X)$ over $\fq$ is either a binomial or a trinomial. We refer to such polynomials $f_n(X)$ as \emph{3-sparse} over $\fq$. This observation naturally leads us to the question of identifying all positive integers $n$ for which $f_n(X)$ is 3-sparse over a given finite field $\fq$. In 2021, Oliveira and Reis \cite{[OR21]} addressed this problem, describing all positive integers $n$ such that $f_n(X)$ is 3-sparse over $\fq$ for $q=2$ and $q=4$. 

However, the problem of determining the $3$-sparsity of $f_n(X)$ over $\fq$ remains open for any odd $q$. In this paper, we prove that for any given odd prime power $q$, any prime $p$ such that $f_p(X)$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_q$ must divides $q^2-1$, thus resolving the problem of Oliveira and Reis for odd characteristic. Applying this result, we completely characterize the $3$-sparsity of $f_n(X)$ over $\fq$ for $q = 3$ and $q = 9$. Moreover, for $q=3$ and $q=9$, if $f_n(X)$ is $3$-sparse over $\fq$, then the explicit irreducible factorization of $f_n(X)$ is given. 

Now we state the main results of this paper. The first theorem establishes a necessary condition on primes $p$ for which $f_p(X)$ is $3$-sparse over finite fields of odd characteristic. It resolves an open problem by Oliveira and Reis \cite[Problem 2]{[OR21]}, which asks whether, for any given $q$, there are only finitely many primes $p$ such that $X^p-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\fq$.
\begin{thm}\label{thm1.3}
Let $q$ be an odd prime power. For a prime $p$, if $X^p-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\fq$, then we have that $p$ divides $q^2-1$. Consequently, for any given odd prime power $q$, there are only finitely many primes $p$ such that $X^p-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\fq$.
\end{thm}


Our second result deals with the case $q=3$.
\begin{thm}\label{thm1.1}
Let $n$ be a positive integer not divisible by $3$ and $\fth=\{-1,0,1\}$ be the finite field with three elements. Then
$X^{n}-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\fth$ if and only if $n=2^k$ for some nonnegative integer $k$. Furthermore, for any integer $k\ge 0$, the irreducible factorization of $X^{2^k}-1$ over $\fth$ is given by
$$X^{2^k}-1=\begin{cases}
X-1,&\text{if}\ k=0,\\
(X-1)(X+1),&\text{if}\ k=1,\\
(X-1)(X+1)(X^2+1),&\text{if}\ k=2,
\end{cases}$$
and 
$$X^{2^k}-1=(X-1)(X+1)(X^2+1)\prod_{i=3}^k\prod_{u\in\fth^*}(X^{2^{i-2}}+uX^{2^{i-3}}-1)$$
if $k\ge 3$.
\end{thm}

Our third result deals with the case $q=9$.
\begin{thm}\label{thm1.2}
Let $n$ be a positive integer not divisible by $3$. Let $\mathbb{F}_9$ be the finite field with nine elements, and write $\mathbb{F}_9=\fth(\alpha)$ with $\alpha^2=-1$. Then 
$X^{n}-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_9$ if and only if $n=2^{k_1}5^{k_2}$ for some nonnegative integers $k_1$ and $k_2$. Moreover, for any integers $k_1, k_2\ge 0$, the irreducible factorization of $X^{2^{k_1}5^{k_2}}-1$ over $\mathbb{F}_9$ is given as follows:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*), left=0pt]
\item If $k_2=0$, then
$$X^{2^{k_1}}-1=\begin{cases}
X-1,&\text{if}\ k_1=0,\\
(X-1)(X+1),&\text{if}\ k_1=1,\\
(X-1)(X+1)(X+\alpha)(X-\alpha),&\text{if}\ k_1=2,
\end{cases}$$
and 
$$X^{2^{k_1}}-1=\prod_{\gamma\in\{\pm 1,\pm\alpha\}}(X+\gamma)\prod_{i=3}^{k_1}\prod_{u,v\in\fth^*}(X^{2^{i-3}}+u\alpha+v),\ \text{if}\ k_1\ge 3.$$
\item If $k_2\ge 1$, then
\begin{align*}
X^{5^{k_2}}-1&=(X-1)\prod_{i=1}^{k_2}\prod_{u\in\fth^*}(X^{2\cdot5^{i-1}}+(u\alpha-1)X^{5^{i-1}}+1),\\
X^{2\cdot5^{k_2}}-1&=(X-1)(X+1)\prod_{i=1}^{k_2}\prod_{u,v\in\fth^*}(X^{2\cdot5^{i-1}}+(u\alpha+v)X^{5^{i-1}}+1),\\
X^{2^2\cdot5^{k_2}}-1&=\prod_{\gamma\in\{\pm 1,\pm\alpha\}}(X+\gamma)\prod_{i=1}^{k_2}F_i(X),\\
X^{2^3\cdot5^{k_2}}-1&=\prod_{\gamma\in\mathbb{F}_9^*}(X+\gamma)\prod_{i=1}^{k_2}F_i(X)G_i(X),
\end{align*}
and 
\begin{align*}
X^{2^{k_1}\cdot5^{k_2}}-1&=\prod_{\gamma\in\{\pm 1,\pm\alpha\}}(X+\gamma)\prod_{j=3}^{k_1}E_j(X)\prod_{i=1}^{k_2}F_i(X)G_i(X)\prod_{k=4}^{k_1}H_{ik}(X),\ \text{if}\ k_1\ge 4,
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
E_j(X)&=\prod_{u,v\in\fth^*}(X^{2^{j-3}}+u\alpha+v),\\
F_i(X)&=\prod_{u,v,w\in\fth^*}(X^{2\cdot5^{i-1}}+(u\alpha+v)X^{5^{i-1}}+w),\\
G_i(X)&=\prod_{\substack{u,v\in\fth^*\\t\in\{0,1\}}}(X^{2\cdot5^{i-1}}+u\alpha^tX^{5^{i-1}}+v\alpha),\\
H_{ik}(X)&=\prod_{(\gamma_1,\gamma_2)\in S}(X^{2^{k-3}\cdot5^{i-1}}+\gamma_1X^{2^{k-4}\cdot 5^{i-1}}+\gamma_2)
\end{align*}
with $S=\{(\pm1,\pm \alpha-1),(\pm(\alpha+1),\alpha\pm 1),(\pm\alpha,\pm \alpha+1),(\pm(\alpha-1),-\alpha\pm 1)\}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{thm}

This article is structured as follows. In Section 2, we introduce key lemmas  which are essential for establishing the main results. The proofs of Theorem \ref{thm1.3}, Theorem \ref{thm1.1} and Theorem \ref{thm1.2} are presented in Sections 3, 4 and 5, respectively.

\section{Lemmas}

In this section, we introduce several lemmas that will be utilized later.

Let $q$ be a prime power and $\mathbb{F}_q$ denote the finite field with $q$ elements. For any positive integer $d$, let $\Phi_d(X)$ denote the $d$-th cyclotomic polynomial over $\mathbb{F}_q$, defined as follows:
$$\Phi_d(X)=\prod_{\substack{i=1\\
\gcd(i,d)=1}}^d(X-\xi^i),$$
where $\xi$ is a primitive $d$th root of unity. Clearly, 
$$\Phi_d(X)=X^{d-1}+X^{d-2}+\cdots+X+1$$
if $d$ is a prime number. The first two lemmas are basic results of cyclotomic polynomials over finite fields.
\begin{lem}\label{lem2.1}
\cite[Exercise 2.57]{[LN97]} Let $r$ be a prime, and let $m,n$ be positive integer. Then
$$ \Phi_{mr^n}(X)=\Phi_{mr}(X^{r^{n-1}}).$$
\end{lem}

\begin{lem}\label{lem2.2}
\cite[Theorem 2.47]{[LN97]} Let $n$ be a positive integer. Then $$X^n-1=\prod_{d\mid n}\Phi_{d}(X).$$
Moreover, each $\Phi_d(X)$ is the product of $\frac{\phi(d)}{\text{ord}_d(q)}$  distinct irreducible monic polynomials in $\fq[X]$ with the same degree $\text{ord}_d(q)$, where $\text{ord}_d(q)$ denotes the multiplicative order of $q$ modulo $d$ and $\phi$ is the Euler totient function. 
\end{lem}

The following well-known result is one of the so-called lifting the exponent lemmas.
\begin{lem}\label{lem2.3} \cite[Proposition 1]{[Bey77]} Let $p$ be a prime and $a\ge 2$ be an integer. Denote by $v_p(n)$ the $p$-adic valuation of the positive integer $n$. The following are true for any positive integer $k$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*), left=0pt]
\item If $p$ is odd and $p\mid (a-1)$, then
$$v_p(a^k-1)=v_p(a-1)+v_p(k).$$
\item If $p=2$ and $a$ is odd, then
$$v_2(a^k-1)=\begin{cases}
v_2(a-1),&\text{if}\ k\ \text{is\ odd},\\
v_2(a^2-1)+v_2(k)-1,&\text{if}\ k\ \text{is\ even}.
\end{cases}$$
\end{enumerate}
\end{lem}

\begin{lem}\label{lem2.4}
The following statements hold:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*), left=0pt]
\item For any positive integer $k$, the multiplicative order of 3 modulo $2^k$ is given by
\[
\text{ord}_{2^k}(3) = \begin{cases} 
1, & \text{if } k = 1, \\
2, & \text{if } k = 2, \\
2^{k-2}, & \text{if } k \geq 3.
\end{cases}
\]
\item For any nonnegative integers $k_1 \geq 0$ and $k_2 \geq 0$, not both zero, the multiplicative order of 9 modulo $2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}$ is given by
\[
\text{ord}_{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}(9) = \begin{cases} 
1, & \text{if } 1 \leq k_1 \leq 2, \, k_2 = 0, \\
2^{k_1-3}, & \text{if } k_1 \geq 3, \, k_2 = 0, \\
2 \cdot 5^{k_2-1}, & \text{if } 0 \leq k_1 \leq 3, \, k_2 \geq 1, \\
2^{k_1-3} \cdot 5^{k_2-1}, & \text{if } k_1 \geq 4, \, k_2 \geq 1.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{enumerate}
\end{lem}

\begin{proof}
% Establishing base cases for part (a)
It is straightforward to verify that $\text{ord}_{2}(3) = 1$ and $\text{ord}_{4}(3) = 2$. For $k \geq 3$, we aim to prove that $\text{ord}_{2^k}(3) = 2^{k-2}$ through the following steps.

% Proving the order using induction
First, we show that 
\begin{equation}\label{c2-1}
3^{2^{k-2}} \equiv 1 \pmod{2^k}
\end{equation}
for $k \geq 3$ using mathematical induction. For the base case $k = 3$, we have $3^{2^{3-2}}\equiv 1 \pmod{8}$, so \eqref{c2-1} holds. Assume the statement holds for $k-1$ with $k\ge 4$, i.e., $3^{2^{k-3}} \equiv 1 \pmod{2^{k-1}}$. Thus, we can write $3^{2^{k-3}} = a \cdot 2^{k-1} + 1$ for some integer $a$. Squaring both sides, we obtain
$$3^{2^{k-2}} = \left(3^{2^{k-3}}\right)^2 = (a \cdot 2^{k-1} + 1)^2 = a^2 \cdot 2^{2k-2} + 2a \cdot 2^{k-1} + 1 \equiv 1 \pmod{2^k},$$
since $2k-2 \geq k$ for $k \geq 4$. Thus, \eqref{c2-1} holds for $k$, completing the induction.

% Proving minimality of the order
Next, we claim that $3^t \not\equiv 1 \pmod{2^k}$ for any integer $t$ with $1 \leq t < 2^{k-2}$. Suppose there exists such a $t$ satisfying $3^t \equiv 1 \pmod{2^k}$, implying $v_2(3^t - 1) \geq k$. By Lemma \ref{lem2.3}(b), we have $v_2(3^t - 1) \leq v_2(8) + v_2(t) - 1 < 3 + (k-3) - 1 = k-1 < k$, a contradiction. Thus, combining this with \eqref{c2-1}, we conclude that $\text{ord}_{2^k}(3) = 2^{k-2}$ for $k \geq 3$, proving part (a).

% Proving part (b) by cases
For part (b), let $k_1, k_2 \geq 0$ be nonnegative integers, not both zero. If $1 \leq k_1 \leq 2$ and $k_2 = 0$, it is clear that $\text{ord}_{2^{k_1}}(9) = 1$. We now consider the remaining cases.

\textbf{Case 1: $k_1 \geq 3$, $k_2 = 0$.} Here, $\text{ord}_{2^{k_1}}(9) = \frac{1}{2} \text{ord}_{2^{k_1}}(3)$. By part (a), $\text{ord}_{2^{k_1}}(3) = 2^{k_1-2}$, so $\text{ord}_{2^{k_1}}(9) = 2^{k_1-3}$.

\textbf{Case 2: $0 \leq k_1 \leq 3$, $k_2 \geq 1$.} We first show that
\begin{equation}\label{c2-2}
9^{2 \cdot 5^{k_2-1}} \equiv 1 \pmod{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}
\end{equation}
using induction on $k_2$. For $k_2 = 1$, compute $9^2 = 81 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}$ and check modulo $2^{k_1}$ (for $k_1 \leq 3$), which holds similarly. Assume \eqref{c2-2} holds for $k_2-1$. Then, $9^{2 \cdot 5^{k_2-2}} \equiv 1 \pmod{5^{k_2-1}}$, and raising to the fifth power gives $9^{2 \cdot 5^{k_2-1}} \equiv 1 \pmod{5^{k_2}}$. For $2^{k_1}$, the order is compatible since $k_1 \leq 3$. Next, we claim that $9^t \not\equiv 1 \pmod{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}$ for $1 \leq t < 2 \cdot 5^{k_2-1}$. If such a $t$ exists, then $v_5(9^t - 1) \geq k_2$ and $t$ is even. By Lemma \ref{lem2.3}(a), $v_5(9^t - 1) = v_5(81^{t/2} - 1) = v_5(80) + v_5(t/2) < 1 + k_2 - 1 = k_2$, a contradiction. Thus, $\text{ord}_{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}(9) = 2 \cdot 5^{k_2-1}$.

\textbf{Case 3: $k_1 \geq 5$, $k_2 \geq 1$.} From \eqref{c2-1}, we have $9^{2^{k_1-3} \cdot 5^{k_2-1}} = (3^{2^{k_1-2}})^{5^{k_2-1}} \equiv 1 \pmod{2^{k_1}}$. From \eqref{c2-2}, $9^{2 \cdot 5^{k_2-1}} \equiv 1 \pmod{5^{k_2}}$, so $9^{2^{k_1-3} \cdot 5^{k_2-1}} = (9^{2 \cdot 5^{k_2-1}})^{2^{k_1-4}} \equiv 1 \pmod{5^{k_2}}$. Thus,
\begin{equation}\label{c2-3}
9^{2^{k_1-3} \cdot 5^{k_2-1}} \equiv 1 \pmod{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}.
\end{equation}
We claim that $9^t \not\equiv 1 \pmod{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}$ for $1 \leq t < 2^{k_1-3} \cdot 5^{k_2-1}$. If such a $t$ exists, then $v_2(9^t - 1) \geq k_1$ and $v_5(9^t - 1) \geq k_2$. Since $t < 2^{k_1-3} \cdot 5^{k_2-1}$, either $v_2(t) < k_1-3$ or $v_5(t) < k_2-1$. If $v_2(t) < k_1-3$, Lemma \ref{lem2.3}(b) gives $v_2(9^t - 1) \leq v_2(80) + v_2(t) - 1 < 4 + (k_1-3) - 1 < k_1$, a contradiction. If $v_5(t) < k_2-1$, then $v_5(9^t - 1) = v_5(81^{t/2} - 1) < k_2$, a contradiction. Thus, $\text{ord}_{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}(9) = 2^{k_1-3} \cdot 5^{k_2-1}$.

Combining all cases, part (b) is proved, completing the proof of Lemma \ref{lem2.4}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lem}\label{lem2.6}
\cite[Theorem 3.39]{[LN97]} Let $f(X)$ be a monic irreducible polynomial in $\fq[X]$ of degree $m$. Let $\alpha$ be a root of $f(X)$ in $\mathbb{F}_{q^m}$, and for positive integer $t$ let $G_t(X)$ be the characteristic polynomial of $\alpha^t\in\mathbb{F}_{q^m}$ over $\fq$. Then
$$G_t(X^t)=(-1)^{m(t+1)}\prod_{j=1}^tf(w_jX),$$
where $w_1,\ldots,w_t$ are the $t$-th roots of unity over $\fq$ counted according to multiplicity.
\end{lem}

\begin{lem}\label{lem2.7}
\cite[Lemma 2]{[BMOV15]} Let $m$ and $n$ be positive integers, and let $a\in\fq^*$ be of order $M$ in the group $\fq^*$. Then $X^m-a$ divides $X^n-1$ if and only if $mM$ divides $n$.
\end{lem}

\section{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.3}}

In this section, we present the proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.3}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.3}]
Let $q$ be an odd prime power. Suppose there exists a prime $p$ not a divisor of $q^2-1$ such that $X^p-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_q$, meaning its irreducible factors are binomials or trinomials. Let $\Phi_p(X)$ denote the $p$-th cyclotomic polynomial over $\mathbb{F}_q$. By Lemma \ref{lem2.2}, $\Phi_p(X)$ divides $X^p-1$, so its irreducible factors over $\mathbb{F}_q$ are either binomials or trinomials.

We claim that $\Phi_p(X)$ has no binomial factors. Suppose $\Phi_p(X)$ has an irreducible factor of the form $X^m - a$, where $1 \leq m \leq p-1$ and $a \in \mathbb{F}_q^*$. By Lemma \ref{lem2.7}, since $X^m - a$ divides $X^p - 1$, the order $M$ of $a$ in $\mathbb{F}_q^*$ satisfies $mM \mid p$. As $M \mid (q-1)$ and $p > p_0$, with $p_0$ the largest prime divisor of $q^2-1 = (q-1)(q+1)$, it follows that $M = 1$ and $m = p$. However, since $m \leq p-1$, this implies $m = 1$ and $M = 1$, which is a contradiction. Thus, $\Phi_p(X)$ has no binomial factors.

By Lemma \ref{lem2.2}, the irreducible factorization of $\Phi_p(X)$ is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c5-1}
\Phi_p(X) = \prod_{i=1}^{(p-1)/t} (X^t + a_i X^{k_i} + b_i),
\end{equation}
where $a_i, b_i \in \mathbb{F}_q^*$, $1 \leq k_i < t$, $t = \text{ord}_p(q)$ is the order of $q$ modulo $p$, and all factors $X^t + a_i X^{k_i} + b_i$ are distinct irreducible trinomials.

Since $\Phi_p(X) = X^{p-1} + \cdots + X + 1$, comparing coefficients in \eqref{eq:c5-1} implies that $\Phi_p(X)$ has an irreducible factor $g(X) = X^t + a X + b$, with $a, b \in \mathbb{F}_q^*$. Let $\xi$ be a root of $g(X)$, a primitive $p$-th root of unity in $\mathbb{F}_{q^t}$. As $p$ is odd, $\xi^2$ is also a primitive $p$-th root of unity. Let $g_2(X)$ be the minimal polynomial of $\xi^2$ over $\mathbb{F}_q$, which is an irreducible factor of $\Phi_p(X)$ of degree $t$.

The characteristic polynomial $G_2(X)$ of $\xi^2 \in \mathbb{F}_{q^t}$ over $\mathbb{F}_q$ satisfies, by Lemma \ref{lem2.7},
\[
G_2(X^2) = (-1)^t g(X) g(-X) =
\begin{cases} 
X^{2t} + 2b X^t - a^2 X^2 + b^2, & \text{if } t \text{ is even}, \\
X^{2t} + 2a X^{t+1} + a^2 X^2 - b^2, & \text{if } t \text{ is odd},
\end{cases}
\]
yielding
\[
G_2(X) =
\begin{cases} 
X^t + 2b X^{t/2} - a^2 X + b^2, & \text{if } t \text{ is even}, \\
X^t + 2a X^{(t+1)/2} + a^2 X - b^2, & \text{if } t \text{ is odd}.
\end{cases}
\]
Since $g_2(X)$ divides $G_2(X)$ and both have degree $t$, it follows that $g_2(X) = G_2(X)$. Note that $t=\text{ord}_p(q) \geq 3$ as $p \nmid (q^2-1)$, and $q$ is odd. This implies that $g_2(X)$ is a quadrinomial. This contradicts the assumption that all irreducible factors of $\Phi_p(X)$ are trinomials.

Thus, for any prime $p$, if $X^p-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_q$, then $p$ must divide $q^2-1$. This completes the proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.3}.
\end{proof}

\section{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.1}}\label{sec:thm1.1}

We now provide a detailed proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.1}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.1}]
First, we establish the necessity. Let $f_n(X)=X^n-1$ be $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_3$  with $\gcd(n, 3)=1$. For $n = 1$, the result is trivial. For $n\geq 2$, let $p$ be any prime divisor of $n$. Clearly, $X^p-1$ divides $X^n-1$. Then $X^p-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_3$. It follows from Theorem \ref{thm1.3} that $p\mid (3^2-1)$, that is, $p=2$. The necessity is proved.

Next for the sufficiency, let $f_n(X) = X^n - 1$, where $n = 2^k$ for some nonnegative integer $k$. We compute the irreducible factorization of $f_{2^k}(X)$ over $\mathbb{F}_3$. For $k \leq 3$, we obtain:
\begin{align*}
f_{2^0}(X) &= X - 1, \\
f_{2^1}(X) &= (X - 1)(X + 1), \\
f_{2^2}(X) &= (X - X1)(X + 1)(X^2 + 1), \\
f_{2^3}(X) &= (X - _1)(X + 1)(X^2 + 1) \prod_{u \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^2 + uX + 2).
\end{align*}
For $k \geq 4$, Lemma \ref{lem2.2} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c3-2}
f_{2^k}(X) = \prod_{i=0}^k \Phi_{2^i}(X) = f_{2^3}(X) \prod_{i=4}^k \Phi_{2^i}(X).
\end{equation}
Note that $\Phi_8(X)$ has the irreducible factorization:
$$\Phi_8(X) = \prod_{u \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^2 + uX + 2).$$
Then, for $4 \leq i \leq k$, Lemma \ref{lem2.1} yields
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c3-3}
\Phi_{2^i}(X) = \Phi_8(X^{2^{i-3}}) = \prod_{u \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^{2^{i-2}} + uX^{2^{i-3}} + 2).
\end{equation}
By Lemmas \ref{lem2.2} and \ref{lem2.4}(a), $\Phi_{2^i}(X)$ splits into two irreducible polynomials of degree $2^{i-2}$ over $\mathbb{F}_3$, verifying that \eqref{eq:c3-3} is indeed the irreducible factorization of $\Phi_{2^i}(X)$. Combining \eqref{eq:c3-2} and \eqref{eq:c3-3}, we get
$$f_{2^k}(X) = (X - 1)(X + 1)(X^2 + 1) \prod_{i=3}^k \prod_{u \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^{2^{i-2}} + uX^{2^{i-3}} + 2).$$
Thus, $f_{2^k}(X)$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_3$, proving sufficiency.

The proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.1} is done.
\end{proof}

\section{Proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.2}}\label{sec:thm1.2}

Here, we establish the proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.2}.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.2}]
We begin with the necessity. Let $f_n(X) = X^n - 1$ be $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_9$, with $\gcd(n, 3) = 1$. For $n = 1$, the necessity holds trivially. For $n \geq 2$, let $p$ be any prime divisor of $n$. We have $X^p-1$ divides $X^n-1$, implying that $X^p-1$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_9$. By Theorem \ref{thm1.3}, it follows that $p\mid (9^2-1)$, i.e., $p=2$ or $p=5$. Therefore, $n=2^{k_1}\cdot 5^{k_2}$ for some integers $k_1$ and $k_2$. The proof of necessity is established.

For sufficiency, let $\mathbb{F}_9 = \mathbb{F}_3(\alpha)$, with $\alpha^2=-1$, and $f_n(X) = X^n - 1$, where $n = 2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}$ for nonnegative integers $k_1, k_2$. We derive the irreducible factorization of $f_n(X)$ over $\mathbb{F}_9$ across several cases.

\textbf{Case 1: $k_2 = 0$.} For $k_1 \leq 3$, we compute:
\begin{align*}
f_{2^0}(X) &= X - 1, \\
f_{2^1}(X) &= (X - 1)(X + 1), \\
f_{2^2}(X) &= (X - 1)(X + 1)(X + \alpha)(X + 2\alpha), \\
f_{2^3}(X) &= \prod_{\gamma \in \{\pm 1, \pm \alpha\}} (X + \gamma) \prod_{u, v \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X + u\alpha + v).
\end{align*}
For $k_1 \geq 4$, Lemma \ref{lem2.2} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c4-2}
f_{2^{k_1}}(X) = \prod_{i=0}^{k_1} \Phi_{2^i}(X) = f_{2^3}(X) \prod_{i=4}^{k_1} \Phi_{2^i}(X).
\end{equation}
The factorization of $\Phi_8(X)$ is
\[
\Phi_8(X) = \prod_{u, v \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X + u\alpha + v).
\]
For $4 \leq i \leq k_1$, Lemma \ref{lem2.1} yields
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c4-3}
\Phi_{2^i}(X) = \Phi_8(X^{2^{i-3}}) = \prod_{u, v \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^{2^{i-3}} + u\alpha + v).
\end{equation}
Lemmas \ref{lem2.2} and \ref{lem2.4}(b) confirm that $\Phi_{2^i}(X)$ splits into polynomials of degree $2^{i-3}$, so \eqref{eq:c4-3} is the irreducible factorization. Combining \eqref{eq:c4-2} and \eqref{eq:c4-3}, we get
\[
f_{2^{k_1}}(X) = \prod_{\gamma \in \{\pm 1, \pm \alpha\}} (X + \gamma) \prod_{i=3}^{k_1} \prod_{u, v \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^{2^{i-3}} + u\alpha + v), \quad \text{if } k_1 \geq 3.
\]

\textbf{Case 2: $k_1 = 0$, $k_2 \geq 1$.} Lemma \ref{lem2.2} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c4-4}
f_{5^{k_2}}(X) = (X - 1) \prod_{i=1}^{k_2} \Phi_{5^i}(X).
\end{equation}
The factorization of $\Phi_5(X)$ is
\[
\Phi_5(X) = \prod_{u \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^2 + (u\alpha + 2)X + 1).
\]
For $1 \leq i \leq k_2$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c4-5}
\Phi_{5^i}(X) = \Phi_5(X^{5^{i-1}}) = \prod_{u \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^{2 \cdot 5^{i-1}} + (u\alpha + 2)X^{5^{i-1}} + 1).
\end{equation}
Lemmas \ref{lem2.2} and \ref{lem2.4} show $\Phi_{5^i}(X)$ factors into $\frac{\phi(5^i)}{2 \cdot 5^{i-1}}$ polynomials of degree $2 \cdot 5^{i-1}$, confirming that \eqref{eq:c4-5} is the irreducible factorization over $\mathbb{F}_9$. Substituting into \eqref{eq:c4-4} gives the factorization of $f_{5^{k_2}}(X)$.

\textbf{Case 3: $k_1 = 1$, $k_2 \geq 1$.} Lemma \ref{lem2.2} yields
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c4-6}
f_{2 \cdot 5^{k_2}}(X) = (X - 1)(X + 1) \prod_{i=1}^{k_2} \Phi_{5^i}(X) \prod_{j=1}^{k_2} \Phi_{2 \cdot 5^j}(X).
\end{equation}
From Case 2, we have the irreducible factorization of $\Phi_{5^i}(X)$. Note that $\Phi_{10}(X)$ factors as
$$\Phi_{10}(X) = \prod_{u \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^2 + (u\alpha + 1)X + 1).$$
By Lemma \ref{lem2.1}, for $1 \leq i \leq k_2$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c4-7}
\Phi_{2 \cdot 5^i}(X) = \Phi_{10}(X^{5^{i-1}}) = \prod_{u \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^{2 \cdot 5^{i-1}} + (u\alpha + 1)X^{5^{i-1}} + 1).
\end{equation}
Lemmas \ref{lem2.2} and \ref{lem2.4} confirm $\Phi_{2 \cdot 5^i}(X)$ factors into $\frac{\phi(2 \cdot 5^i)}{2 \cdot 5^{i-1}}$ polynomials of degree $2 \cdot 5^{i-1}$. It follows that \eqref{eq:c4-7} is the irreducible factorization over $\mathbb{F}_9$. Substituting \eqref{eq:c4-5} and \eqref{eq:c4-7} into \eqref{eq:c4-6} gives the factorization of $f_{2 \cdot 5^{k_2}}(X)$.

\textbf{Case 4: $2 \leq k_1 \leq 3$, $k_2 \geq 1$.} Similar to Case 3, we obtain:
\begin{align*}
f_{2^2 \cdot 5^{k_2}}(X) &= \prod_{\gamma \in \{\pm 1, \pm \alpha\}} (X + \gamma) \prod_{i=1}^{k_2} F_i(X), \\
f_{2^3 \cdot 5^{k_2}}(X) &= \prod_{\gamma \in \mathbb{F}_9^*} (X + \gamma) \prod_{i=1}^{k_2} F_i(X) G_i(X),
\end{align*}
where
\begin{align*}
F_i(X) &= \prod_{u, v, w \in \mathbb{F}_3^*} (X^{2 \cdot 5^{i-1}} + (u\alpha + v)X^{5^{i-1}} + w), \\
G_i(X) &= \prod_{\substack{u, v \in \mathbb{F}_3^* \\ t \in \{0, 1\}}} (X^{2 \cdot 5^{i-1}} + u\alpha^t X^{5^{i-1}} + v\alpha).
\end{align*}

\textbf{Case 5: $k_1 \geq 4$, $k_2 \geq 1$.} Lemma \ref{lem2.2} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c4-8}
f_{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}(X) = f_{2^{k_1}}(X) \left( \prod_{j=0}^{3} \prod_{i=1}^{k_2} \Phi_{2^j \cdot 5^i}(X) \right) \left( \prod_{j=4}^{k_1} \prod_{i=1}^{k_2} \Phi_{2^j \cdot 5^i}(X) \right).
\end{equation}
From prior cases, we have factorizations for $f_{2^{k_1}}(X)$ and $\Phi_{2^j \cdot 5^i}(X)$ for $0 \leq j \leq 3$ and $1\le i\le k_2$. From Case 4, we know that $\Phi_{80}(X)$ factors as
$$\Phi_{80}(X) = \prod_{(\gamma_1, \gamma_2) \in S} (X^2 + \gamma_1 X + \gamma_2),$$
where $S = \{ (\pm 1, \pm \alpha - 1), (\pm (\alpha + 1), \alpha \pm 1), (\pm \alpha, \pm \alpha + 1), (\pm (\alpha - 1), -\alpha \pm 1) \}$. For $4 \leq j \leq k_1$, $1 \leq i \leq k_2$, Lemma \ref{lem2.1} gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c4-9}
\Phi_{2^j \cdot 5^i}(X) = \Phi_{2^4 \cdot 5}(X^{2^{j-4} \cdot 5^{i-1}}) = \prod_{(\gamma_1, \gamma_2) \in S} (X^{2^{j-3} \cdot 5^{i-1}} + \gamma_1 X^{2^{j-4} \cdot 5^{i-1}} + \gamma_2).
\end{equation}
Lemmas \ref{lem2.2} and \ref{lem2.4} confirm $\Phi_{2^j \cdot 5^i}(X)$ factors into $\frac{\phi(2^j \cdot 5^i)}{2^{j-3} \cdot 5^{i-1}}$ polynomials of degree $2^{j-3} \cdot 5^{i-1}$, implying that \eqref{eq:c4-9} is the irreducible factorization. Substituting \eqref{eq:c4-9} into \eqref{eq:c4-8} gives the irreducible factorization of $f_{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}(X)$.

Thus, $f_{2^{k_1} \cdot 5^{k_2}}(X)$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_9$, completing the proof of sufficiency.

This completes the proof of Theorem \ref{thm1.2}.
\end{proof}

\section{Conclusion}

In this paper, we have made significant strides in addressing the problem of characterizing the $3$-sparsity of the binomial $f_n(X) = X^n - 1$ over finite fields of odd characteristic. A key contribution of this work is the resolution of the open problem posed by Oliveira and Reis for the case of odd characteristic. We have affirmatively answered this question, see Theorem \ref{thm1.3}. By using this result, we extended the results of Oliveira and Reis \cite{[OR21]} by determining all positive integers $n$ for which $f_n(X)$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_3$ and $\mathbb{F}_9$, where $n$ is not divisible by $3$.

% Highlighting the general approach and its effectiveness
Moreover, the methodology developed in this paper offers a more general and precise framework for identifying all positive integers $n$ such that $f_n(X)$ is $3$-sparse over $\mathbb{F}_q$ for any given odd prime power $q$. By using cyclotomic polynomials, multiplicative orders, and properties of finite fields, our approach systematically constrains the possible prime divisors of $n$ and constructs the corresponding irreducible factorizations. We believe that this method is particularly effective for small values of odd $q$, like $q=27,81,5,25,125,7,49$, as demonstrated by our comprehensive results for $q =3$ and $q=9$. 

