\subsection{Ablation Study}
To validate the effectiveness of our proposed components, we conduct ablation studies on the H100 GPU.

\subsubsection{The effectiveness of workload distribution.}
\label{sec:threshold_eva}
We test 500 matrices in three computing patterns in Libra: CUDA-core-only and TCU-only (both without workload distribution), and Hybrid-computation.
For each matrix, we identify the pattern that delivers the best performance.
In SpMM, Hybrid-computation achieves the fastest performance on 328 matrices, while in SDDMM, it is the fastest on 453 matrices. Based on these matrices, Table~\ref{tab:ab_hybrid} presents the speedup distribution of Hybrid-computation over CUDA-core-only and TCU-only patterns.
For SpMM, Hybrid-computation achieves an average speedup of $1.59\times$ over CUDA-core-only and $1.22\times$ over TCU-only. 
For SDDMM, it provides an average speedup of $1.97\times$ over CUDA-core-only and $1.21\times$ over TCU-only. 
These results validate that our 2D-aware workload distribution strategy can effectively guide workload distribution, enabling efficient hybrid computation.

Furthermore, the threshold selection is crucial for 2D-aware workload distribution strategy to find out the sweet point of task mapping.
For SpMM, the threshold varies from 1 to 8 for an 8$\times$1 vector, whereas for SDDMM, it ranges from 1 to 128 for an 8$\times$16 TC block. 
% It is uncommon for more than half of the elements in an 8$\times$16 TC block to be non-zero in sparse matrices, 
In SDDMM, we evaluated 8 threshold values commonly used in practice, ranging from 8 to 64 in increments of 8.
We selected matrices that exhibit diverse sparsity patterns and notable hybrid acceleration. Using these matrices, we further evaluate how the performance of Hybrid-computation pattern changes with different threshold selections.
Figure~\ref{fig:threshold} presents the optimal threshold selection across different sparse matrices, GPU architectures and sparse operators. 
The optimal threshold (denoted by a star) delivers the highest speedup, with 3 for SpMM and 24 for SDDMM.
This indicates that similar threshold selection are effective across different matrices, empirically validating our theoretical analysis in Section~\ref{sec:practice}. 
For a given architecture, the threshold only needs to be determined once and can be reused across different matrices. Consequently, compared with existing approaches~\cite{pcgcn,ye2023sparsetir} that require extensive manual tuning with numerous parameters, our 2D-aware workload distribution strategy precisely identifies optimal task mapping with negligible tuning overhead.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
