\subsubsection{The effectiveness of load balancing.}
We evaluate the performance improvement by incorporating the load balancing in hybrid computation.
The parameters $T_s$ and $C_s$ are set to 32, and $Short\_len$ is set to 3, empirically.
As shown in Table~\ref{tab:ab_format}, we achieve performance improvements in 212 matrices (most with power-law distribution), with 67.3\% of them achieving a speedup greater than $1.2\times$ (averaging $7.2\times$ speedup).
This improvement is driven by even task mapping across thread blocks while minimizing atomic overhead.

